The same assistant is available as a page, `AskController`, which is easier to read than a long terminal answer. Register two URLs in `routes/web.php`. The page posts to its own path plus `/query`, so it works at whatever address you choose:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Controllers\AskController;

Router::get('/chat', [AskController::class, 'index']);
Router::post('/chat/query', [AskController::class, 'query']);
```

Each question shows its answer (when AI is available) and the matching sections as collapsible cards with a link to the full page. The page is a development tool: both URLs respond with a 404 unless `app_env` is `development`, so it is safe to leave registered when you deploy.

On the error page the model may propose up to three changes, each as replace this exact text with that. Nothing is written when the answer arrives. Each proposal is checked first and discarded, with a note, if:

- it names a file that was not part of the message, or is in `vendor/`, `config/`, `storage/` or is a `.env` file;
- the text to replace is not found, or is found more than once (so there is nothing to guess);
- it contains a masked `[REDACTED]` value;
- it would turn a PHP file that parses into one that does not.

What survives is shown as a diff. Apply this change needs two clicks (the first arms the button), re-checks the file, saves a copy of the original at `storage/debug/assist/undo/<id>.orig`, writes the change and keeps your line endings, then offers to reload the page. Copy diff lets you apply it yourself. The browser only ever sends the proposal's id back, so a request cannot make the server write anything the model did not propose.

The assistant is built on `AiClientInterface`, which you can type-hint in any container-resolved class. It has two methods, and both return an `AiResponse`:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\AiClientInterface;

class SummaryController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(protected AiClientInterface $ai) {}

    public function summarize(string $text): string
    {
        $response = $this->ai->generateContent('Summarize: ' . $text, [
            'system'      => 'Reply in two sentences.',
            'temperature' => 0.2,
            'max_tokens'  => 200,
        ]);

        return $response->getText();
    }
}
```

Option | Meaning
`system` | Standing instructions, kept apart from the user prompt. Every provider implementation must honor it.
`model` | Override the configured model for this call.
`temperature`, `top_p`, `top_k` | Sampling controls.
`max_tokens` | Maximum length of the reply.
`timeout` | Request timeout in seconds.

For a conversation, pass an ordered list of turns to `generateChatContent()`, each with a `role` of `user` or `assistant` and a `content` string.

Implement `AiClientInterface` and rebind it in your app's `bootstrap.php`. It is on the list of core bindings an app may replace, and the assistant picks up your client automatically:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Bootstrap\ServiceRegistrar;
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\AiClientInterface;

return static function (ServiceRegistrar $services): void {
    $services->singleton(AiClientInterface::class, fn () => new MyOtherProviderClient());
};
```

If you want to add custom functionality (e.g., additional fields, custom validation, or custom email templates), you can extend the core controller:

```php
// app/Controllers/AuthController.php
namespace App\Controllers;

use Rhapsody\Core\Controllers\AuthController as BaseAuthController;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;

class AuthController extends BaseAuthController
{
    public function showRegisterForm(): Response
    {
        // Add custom data to the registration view
        return $this->view('auth/register.twig', [
            'terms_accepted' => false,
        ]);
    }

    public function register(Request $request): Response
    {
        // Add custom validation before parent registration
        // Or override the entire registration logic
        // return parent::register($request);
    }
}
```

Then update your `routes/web.php` to point to your custom controller:

```php
Router::get('/register', [App\Controllers\AuthController::class, 'showRegisterForm']);
Router::post('/register', [App\Controllers\AuthController::class, 'register']);
```

To add custom fields to the user (e.g., `phone`, `avatar`), you can extend the core entity:

```php
// app/Entities/User.php
namespace App\Entities;

use Doctrine\ORM\Mapping as ORM;
use Rhapsody\Core\Entities\User as BaseUser;

#[ORM\Entity]
#[ORM\Table(name: 'users')]
class User extends BaseUser
{
    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', nullable: true)]
    private ?string $phone = null;

    public function getPhone(): ?string
    {
        return $this->phone;
    }

    public function setPhone(?string $phone): void
    {
        $this->phone = $phone;
    }
}
```

Note: If you create your own `App\Entities\User` entity, it will override the core `User` entity. Make sure your entity contains all the fields the auth system expects, or extend the base entity as shown above.

If the built-in authentication system doesn't meet your needs, you can build your own from scratch. This is recommended if you need:

- Integration with external authentication providers (LDAP, OAuth)
- Non-email based login (e.g., username only)
- Custom session management
- Different user storage (e.g., Redis, external API)

To build your own system, you can either:

1. Create a completely separate auth controller – Create your own `LoginController` and `RegisterController` in `app/Controllers/` and use your own routes.
2. Create independent login/register controllers – This is the recommended approach as it keeps responsibilities separated.

```php
// app/Controllers/LoginController.php
namespace App\Controllers;

use Rhapsody\Core\BaseController;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;
use Rhapsody\Core\Session;

class LoginController extends BaseController
{
    public function showForm(): Response
    {
        return $this->view('auth/custom-login.twig');
    }

    public function login(Request $request): Response
    {
        // Your custom login logic here
        // Validate credentials, set session, etc.
    }

    public function logout(): Response
    {
        Session::destroy();
        return redirect('/login');
    }
}
```

Then register your routes in `routes/web.php`:

```php
Router::get('/login', [LoginController::class, 'showForm']);
Router::post('/login', [LoginController::class, 'login']);
Router::post('/logout', [LoginController::class, 'logout']);
```

This method is a "cache-aside" implementation that simplifies the process of getting and setting cache items:

1. It checks if a value exists in the cache for a given key.
2. If it exists and is not expired, it returns the cached value.
3. If it does not exist, it executes the provided function, saves its return value to the cache for a specified number of minutes, and then returns that value.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Cache;

class PostController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(protected Cache $cache, /*...*/) {}

    public function index()
    {
        // This query will only run if 'posts.all' is not in the cache
        // or has expired. Otherwise, the result is fetched directly from
        // the cache (file or Redis), avoiding a database hit.
        $posts = $this->cache->remember('posts.all', 60, function () {
            // Expensive operation, like a database query
            return $this->postModel->findAll();
        });

        return $this->view('posts/index.twig', ['posts' => $posts]);
    }
}
```

This method works seamlessly regardless of which driver is active.

A driver is any class that implements `Rhapsody\Core\Cache\CacheInterface`. The value-carrying methods use native `mixed` types, and your signatures must match:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Cache\CacheInterface;

class MyDriver implements CacheInterface
{
    public function get(string $key, mixed $default = null): mixed { /* ... */ }
    public function put(string $key, mixed $value, int $minutes): void { /* ... */ }
    public function has(string $key): bool { /* ... */ }
    public function forget(string $key): void { /* ... */ }
    public function flush(): bool { /* ... */ }
}
```

Values from `config.php` can be read through `Rhapsody\Core\Support\Config`, a typed reader with dot notation. It does for the config array what `Env` does for environment variables, with one big difference: it fails loudly. A missing key or a value of the wrong type throws a `RuntimeException` at the point of use, instead of letting a bad value travel through your code.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Config;

$config = $container->get(Config::class);

$host  = $config->string('mail.host');          // throws if 'mail.host' is missing
$port  = $config->int('mail.port', 587);        // 587 when the key is missing
$debug = $config->bool('app.debug', false);
$items = $config->array('cache.stores', []);

$raw = $config->get('app.name', 'Rhapsody');    // any type; the default when missing

if ($config->has('mail.host')) {
    // the key exists
}
```

The keys above are examples; use the keys defined in your own `config.php`.

`bind()` tells the container: "When someone asks for this abstract name, build it like this." A new instance is created on every `resolve()` call. Use this for things that shouldn't be shared, like a per-request `Request` object.

```php
// Bind a class to a factory function
$container->bind(Request::class, fn() => new Request());

// Bind an interface to a concrete implementation
$container->bind(LoggerInterface::class, FileLogger::class);

// Bind a string alias to a factory function
$container->bind('db', function () {
    return new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=test', 'user', 'pwd');
});
```

**Binding to a class name (aliases)**

When the second argument is a class name rather than a closure, the container treats the binding as an alias: asking for the abstract resolves the class you named, auto-wiring its constructor as usual.

- The target's own binding is honored. If `FileLogger::class` is itself bound (a closure, a `singleton()`, an `instance()`), that binding is what you get back.
- Aliases can chain. `A` → `B` → `C` resolves `C`. A loop ( `A` → `B` → `A`) throws the usual circular dependency error.
- `singleton()` caches under the abstract. `singleton(LoggerInterface::class, FileLogger::class)` builds one `FileLogger` and returns it for every later `resolve(LoggerInterface::class)`; `resolved()` and `forgetSingleton()` use the abstract name too.
- Self-binding is unchanged. `bind(UserService::class)` with no second argument resolves `UserService` itself.

`singleton()` works exactly like `bind()`, except the first `resolve()` call builds the instance and caches it. Every call after that returns the same object instead of running the factory again.

Use this for anything expensive or stateful that should be shared for the life of the container — a database connection, the Twig environment, an event dispatcher.

```php
$container->singleton(EntityManager::class, function ($container) {
    return new EntityManager($connection, $config);
});
```

If you've already constructed the object yourself and just want the container to return that exact instance, use `instance()`:

```php
$logger = new Logger($config);
$container->instance(LoggerInterface::class, $logger);
// Every resolve(LoggerInterface::class) call now returns this exact object
```

`resolve()` (also available as `get()` — they're identical) builds and returns an instance of the given abstract:

```php
$logger = $container->resolve(Logger::class);
$logger = $container->get(Logger::class); // same thing
```

If you want to check whether something has been explicitly bound before trying to resolve it, use `has()`:

```php
if ($container->has('db')) {
    $connection = $container->get('db');
}
```

If a class was never explicitly bound, `resolve()` falls back to reflection: it looks at the class's constructor and, for each parameter, tries to resolve that parameter's type automatically.

The rules are simple:

- Type-hinted with a class or interface → resolved recursively.
- Has a default value (like `$debug = false`) → that default is used.
- Anything else → an exception is thrown, telling you exactly which parameter couldn't be resolved and where.

```php
class UserService
{
    public function __construct(
        private LoggerInterface $logger,
        private UserRepository $repository
    ) {}
}

// LoggerInterface and UserRepository are both resolved automatically.
// LoggerInterface needs a binding (see above); UserRepository doesn't,
// as long as its own constructor has no unresolvable dependencies.
$userService = $container->resolve(UserService::class);
```

Heads up: interfaces must be explicitly bound. PHP can't instantiate an interface on its own, so the container has no way to guess which concrete class you meant. `bind(LoggerInterface::class, FileLogger::class)` is what makes the example above work.

`call()` invokes a callable and resolves its parameters from the container, so a method can ask for the services it needs without receiving them in the constructor. This is how queued jobs get their dependencies in `handle()`.

```php
// An instance method: services come from the container
$container->call([$job, 'handle']);

// A class name: the container builds the instance (constructor included) first
$container->call([ReportController::class, 'show']);

// A closure, an invokable object, or a static method
$container->call(fn(Mailer $mailer) => $mailer->flush());
$container->call(new SendDigest());
$container->call([Reports::class, 'build']);

// The return value is passed straight back
$total = $container->call([$invoice, 'total']);
```

Anything the container can't know — an ID from the URL, a flag — goes in the second argument, keyed by parameter name. Named values win over container resolution, and anything not named is resolved as usual:

```php
class ReportController
{
    public function show(int $id, ReportService $reports, string $format = 'html'): Response { /* ... */ }
}

// $id comes from you, ReportService from the container, $format keeps its default
$container->call([ReportController::class, 'show'], ['id' => 42]);
```

If class `A` depends on `B`, and `B` depends back on `A`, resolving either one throws immediately rather than recursing forever:

```php
// Throws: Circular dependency detected: App\A → App\B → App\A
$container->resolve(App\A::class);
```

The same check covers alias bindings: `bind(A::class, B::class)` together with `bind(B::class, A::class)` is reported as a circular dependency.

Every resolution — successful or not — is logged with timing and caller information. This is useful for hunting down slow or unexpected instantiations:

```php
$trace = Container::getTrace();
foreach ($trace as $entry) {
    echo "Resolved {$entry['class']} in {$entry['duration']}ms (called by {$entry['called_by']})\n";
}

Container::resetTrace();        // clear the log
Container::setProxyMode(true);  // flag subsequent entries as proxy resolutions
```

An alias binding produces one entry for the abstract and further entries for the class it points to (and that class's dependencies). The abstract's `duration` includes the time spent resolving the target, so it will always be at least as large as the target's own entry.

For the vast majority of classes, the container should be threaded in through the constructor — that's what auto-wiring is for, and it's what keeps a class's real dependencies visible and easy to test.

But some infrastructure-level classes are routinely instantiated directly with `new` by application code (never resolved through the container itself) and only occasionally need to reach a bound service. The Pagination class rendering a custom Twig template is a good example. Forcing every caller of `new Pagination(...)` to also pass in a `Twig\Environment` just for that one optional feature would be worse than the alternative: a global accessor.

```php
// End of bootstrap.php, after every bind()/singleton() call has run:
Container::setInstance($container);

// Anywhere else in the framework:
$twig = Container::getInstance()->get(Twig\Environment::class);

// Check without triggering an exception if nothing has been set yet:
if (Container::hasInstance()) { ... }

// Test isolation — clear the registered instance between tests:
Container::forgetInstance();
```

Important: call `setInstance()` at the end of `bootstrap.php`, after every `bind()` and `singleton()` call has run. Registering too early is the same footgun that previously caused the custom 404 page to render without Twig bound — a bare, unconfigured container captured before its bindings existed.

Here is a basic example of a controller class.

```php
view('home/index.twig', [
            'title' => 'Welcome',
        ]);
    }

    public function showUsers(Request $request): Response
    {
        $users = (new User())->findAll();

        return $this->view('home/users.twig', [
            'users' => $users,
        ]);
    }
}
```

The `view()` helper renders a Twig template and returns a `Response` object with status 200.

```php
public function dashboard(): Response
{
    return $this->view('dashboard/index.twig', [
        'stats' => $this->getStats(),
    ], [
        'title'       => 'Dashboard',
        'description' => 'Your personal dashboard',
    ]);
}
```

The third parameter allows you to pass SEO metadata (`title`, `description`) that will be automatically merged into the view's `meta` variable.

The `json()` helper creates a JSON response with the correct `Content-Type` header.

```php
public function getUser(string $id): Response
{
    $user = (new User())->findById($id);

    if (!$user) {
        return $this->json(['error' => 'User not found'], 404);
    }

    return $this->json($user);
}
```

The router automatically injects an instance of the `Rhapsody\Core\Request` object as the first parameter to every controller action. This object provides a clean API for accessing request data.

```php
public function handleContact(Request $request): Response
{
    // Get the request method (e.g., 'post')
    $method = $request->getMethod();

    // Get all input (POST, JSON, PUT, etc.)
    $data = $request->getBody();

    // Get a specific query parameter from the URL
    $page = $request->getQueryParam('page', 1);

    // Get a specific POST value
    $email = $request->post('email');

    // Access uploaded files
    $files = $request->getFiles();

    // ...
}
```

The `BaseController` automatically registers the `RoutingExtension` with Twig, making the `route()` function available in all templates.

```twig
<a href="{{ route('profile.show', {id: user.id}) }}">View Profile</a>
```

In PHP (e.g., inside a controller), you can use `Router::generateUrl()`:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Routing\Router;

public function redirectToProfile(Request $request): Response
{
    $url = Router::generateUrl('profile.show', ['id' => $request->get('id')]);
    return redirect($url);
}
```

The container resolves your controllers and their dependencies automatically. If you type‑hint a service in your controller's constructor, it will be injected.

```php
namespace App\Controllers;

use App\Services\PaymentService;
use Rhapsody\Core\BaseController;

class CheckoutController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(
        private PaymentService $paymentService
    ) {}

    public function process(): Response
    {
        $this->paymentService->charge(...);
        // ...
    }
}
```

For performance, controllers are not lazy‑loaded (they are resolved eagerly). However, all other services injected into controllers can be lazy‑loaded if you enable the `LAZY_LOADING_ENABLED` setting in your `.env` file. This means that heavy services (like the ORM or mailer) are only instantiated when they are actually used.

The Services panel in the debug toolbar shows which services were resolved and whether they were lazy‑loaded – perfect for performance tuning.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Storage\Cookie;
use Rhapsody\Core\Storage\SameSite;

// Set a cookie (expires in 1 day)
Cookie::set('theme', 'dark', 86400);

// Set a cookie with custom SameSite
Cookie::set('preferences', ['notifications' => true], 3600, '/', null, true, true, SameSite::Strict);

// Get a cookie value
$theme = Cookie::get('theme', 'light');

// Check if a cookie exists
if (Cookie::has('user_id')) {
    // ...
}

// Delete a cookie
Cookie::delete('theme');

// Get all cookies (decrypted and decoded)
$all = Cookie::all();
```

The `Cookie::set()` method accepts the following parameters (all with secure defaults):

- `name` – the cookie name
- `value` – can be a string, array, or any JSON-serializable value
- `expiry` – lifetime in seconds (0 = session cookie)
- `path` – cookie path (default: `/`)
- `domain` – optional domain
- `secure` – `true` (HTTPS only) by default
- `httpOnly` – `true` (no JavaScript access) by default
- `sameSite` – `SameSite::Lax`, `Strict`, or `None` (enum)

The following Twig functions are available to work with cookies directly in your templates:

Function | Description
`cookie_get(name, default)` | Returns the value of a cookie, or the default if not set.
`cookie_set(name, value, expiry, path, domain, secure, httpOnly, sameSite)` | Sets a cookie (returns nothing, outputs a `Set-Cookie` header).
`cookie_has(name)` | Returns `true` if the cookie exists.
`cookie_delete(name, path, domain)` | Deletes the cookie.

**Example in Twig**

```twig
{# Check if the user has a preference #}
{% if cookie_has('theme') %}
    {% set theme = cookie_get('theme') %}
{% else %}
    {% set theme = 'light' %}
{% endif %}

{# Set a cookie after a form submission (e.g., from a controller) #}
{{ cookie_set('user_id', user.id, 86400) }}
```

All methods return a `<script>` tag ready to be output in your views.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Storage\LocalStorage;

// Generate a script that sets a localStorage item
echo LocalStorage::set('user_preferences', ['theme' => 'dark']);

// Generate a script that gets an item and calls a callback
echo LocalStorage::get('user_id', 'handleUserId');

// Remove an item
echo LocalStorage::remove('temp_data');

// Clear all localStorage
echo LocalStorage::clear();
```

The following Twig functions output `<script>` tags directly. Use them with the `{{ ... }}` syntax (not `|raw`, as they are already safe).

Function | Description
`localstorage_set(key, value, jsonEncode = true)` | Sets a `localStorage` item. If `jsonEncode` is `true`, the value is JSON-encoded.
`localstorage_remove(key)` | Removes a `localStorage` item.
`localstorage_clear()` | Clears all `localStorage` data.

**Example in Twig**

```twig
{# Store the user's theme preference client-side #}
{{ localstorage_set('theme', 'dark') }}

{# Remove a temporary flag #}
{{ localstorage_remove('onboarding_seen') }}
```

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Database;

// Get the global instance
$db = Database::getInstance($config);
$pdo = $db->getConnection();

// Execute a query
$stmt = $pdo->query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1");
$user = $stmt->fetch();
```

```php
$pdo = Database::getInstance($config)->getConnection();
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = :email");
$stmt->execute([':email' => 'user@example.com']);
$user = $stmt->fetch();
```

The `TraceablePDO` class extends the native PDO and logs all queries for debugging purposes. The debug toolbar displays these logs in the Database panel.

To access the query log programmatically:

```php
$queries = TraceablePDO::getQueryLog();
foreach ($queries as $query) {
    echo $query['sql'] . ' (' . $query['executionMS'] . 'ms)';
}
```

This is the most commonly used debugging tool. It prints one or more variables in a beautifully formatted way and then stops script execution immediately.

```php
// In a controller
public function index(Request $request): Response
{
    $user = $this->userModel->findById(1);
    dd($user, $request->getBody()); // Execution stops here
}
```

Output (using Symfony VarDumper) will look like:

```php
App\Models\User {#123 ▼
  +db: PDO {#45 ...}
  #user_id: "1"
  #name: "John Doe"
  #email: "john@example.com"
}
array:2 [▼
  "name" => "Jane"
  "email" => "jane@example.com"
]
```

Same as `dd()`, but execution continues afterwards. Useful when you want to inspect intermediate values without breaking the page.

```php
$result = processData($input);
d($result); // Dump and continue
// More code runs...
```

`defer()` runs a piece of work after the response has been delivered. The visitor gets their page as soon as your controller returns; sending the email, calling the slow API or rebuilding the cache happens afterwards, without making anyone wait.

```php
public function register(Request $request): Response
{
    $user = $this->users->create($request);

    // Runs after the response is sent. The visitor does not wait for it.
    defer(function () use ($user): void {
        $this->welcomeEmail->sendTo($user);
    });

    return $this->view('auth/registered.twig');
}
```

There are two equivalent ways in. Use whichever fits the code you are writing.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\DeferrerInterface;

// 1. The global helper, available everywhere once the app has booted
defer(fn () => $this->rebuildSitemap());

// 2. Inject the service (easier to test and to swap)
public function __construct(private DeferrerInterface $deferrer) {}

$this->deferrer->defer(fn () => $this->rebuildSitemap());
```

New apps from the skeleton have this wired up. If you are updating an existing project, add one line after the response is sent in each entry point:

```php
// index.php
$response->send();
$kernel->afterResponse();
```

```php
// worker.php, inside the while() loop, after the try/catch that responds
$kernel->afterResponse();
```

Apps that do not call `defer()` pay essentially nothing for this: `afterResponse()` returns immediately when nothing was queued.

```php
<?php
namespace App\Entities;
use Doctrine\ORM\Mapping as ORM;

#[ORM\Entity]
#[ORM\Table(name: 'users')]
class User
{
    #[ORM\Id]
    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', length: 255)]
    private string $user_id;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', length: 50)]
    private string $name;

    // ... other properties
}
```

All interactions with the database in Doctrine are handled by the `EntityManager`. Rhapsody's service container is already configured to automatically build and inject the `EntityManager` into your controllers' constructors whenever you type-hint it.

```php
use App\Entities\User;
use Doctrine\ORM\EntityManager; // Import the EntityManager

class AuthController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(
        protected EntityManager $em, // The container injects it here
        protected Validator $validator,
        Environment $twig
    ) {
        parent::__construct($twig);
    }
}
```

You can retrieve entities from the database using repositories.

```php
// Find a single user by their email address
$user = $this->em->getRepository(User::class)->findOneBy(['email' => $email]);

// Find a user by their primary key
$user = $this->em->find(User::class, $userId);
```

To save a new entity or update an existing one, you use the `persist()` and `flush()` methods.

```php
// Create a new User entity
$user = new User();
$user->setName('New User');
$user->setEmail('new@example.com');
$user->setPassword('a-secure-password');

// 1. Tell Doctrine to "manage" this new object
$this->em->persist($user);

// 2. Execute the INSERT query to save it to the database
$this->em->flush();
```

Type-hint `EncrypterInterface` in a controller or service and the container will inject it, or use the shared instance anywhere:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\EncrypterInterface;
use Rhapsody\Core\Services\Encrypter;

// Via dependency injection
public function __construct(protected EncrypterInterface $encrypter) {}

// Or the shared instance
$encrypter = Encrypter::getInstance();

$token = $encrypter->encrypt('my secret value');
// "AQ3x...": safe to use in cookies, URLs and form fields

$plain = $encrypter->decrypt($token);
// "my secret value", or null if it was tampered with or can't be decrypted
```

`decrypt()` returns `null` on any failure, so always compare with `=== null`. An empty string is a valid decrypted value.

Values are strings. To encrypt an array or object, `json_encode()` it first and `json_decode()` the result.

Signing leaves the value readable but makes any change detectable. Use it when the user may see the data and you only need to know it hasn't been altered.

```php
$signed = $encrypter->sign('user:42');
// "user:42.Zr8k..."

$value = $encrypter->unsign($signed);
// "user:42", or null if the value or signature was changed

// Detached signatures, if you want to store the signature separately
$sig = $encrypter->signature($data, 'my-context');
$ok  = $encrypter->verifySignature($data, $sig, 'my-context');
```

`UrlSigner` adds a `signature` query parameter to a URL, and optionally an `expires` timestamp that is covered by the signature. It is ideal for email verification, unsubscribe links and temporary downloads.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Services\UrlSigner;

public function __construct(protected UrlSigner $signer) {}

// Valid until changed
$url = $this->signer->sign('https://example.com/unsubscribe?user=42');

// Valid for one hour
$url = $this->signer->sign('https://example.com/download?file=7', 3600);

// In the route that receives the link: pass the full URL of the request
if (! $this->signer->verify($fullRequestUrl)) {
    // invalid signature, altered parameters, or expired
}
```

Sign and verify the same form of URL. A link signed as a relative path (`/verify?id=5`) will not verify when checked as an absolute URL.

The `encrypted` Doctrine type encrypts a value when it is saved and decrypts it when it is loaded, so the database only ever contains ciphertext. The type is registered by the framework; just use it on an entity property:

```php
use Doctrine\ORM\Mapping as ORM;

#[ORM\Entity]
class Integration
{
    #[ORM\Column(type: 'encrypted', nullable: true)]
    private ?string $apiKey = null;

    public function getApiKey(): ?string { return $this->apiKey; }
    public function setApiKey(?string $key): void { $this->apiKey = $key; }
}
```

The column is created as `TEXT` on MySQL, SQL Server, PostgreSQL and SQLite. In a hand-written migration, use a text column.

Things to know:

- You cannot query by an encrypted column. Each encryption uses a random nonce, so the same plaintext produces different ciphertext every time. `WHERE`, `ORDER BY` and indexes won't work on it. If you need lookups, store a separate hash column.
- Strings only. `json_encode()` arrays before assigning them.
- Unreadable data throws. If a stored value can't be decrypted (corrupt, or the key changed without being added to `APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS`), loading the entity throws an exception instead of returning an empty value that could be saved over the real one.

The default driver is one class behind a small interface. To use something different (for example a key held in a KMS or HSM), implement `Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\EncrypterInterface` and register it, typically in your application's `bootstrap.php`:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\EncrypterInterface;
use Rhapsody\Core\Services\Encrypter;

$myDriver = new App\Security\KmsEncrypter();

Encrypter::setInstance($myDriver);
$container->instance(EncrypterInterface::class, $myDriver);
```

Cookies, signed URLs and encrypted columns all use whichever instance is registered. `Encrypter::setInstance()` is also the right way to supply a fixed key in unit tests.

For more control, you can throw `Rhapsody\Core\Exceptions\HttpException` (optional class – you need to create it). Example:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Exceptions\HttpException;

public function show(string $id): Response
{
    $post = $this->postModel->findById($id);
    if (!$post) {
        throw new HttpException(404, 'Post not found.');
    }
    // ...
}
```

The error handler will then use the provided status code and message, and show the corresponding error view (if exists).

Even without errors, you can write to the same log file using the `Logger` class:

```php
$logger = new \Core\Logger($config['logging']['error_log_path']);
$logger->log('User 123 logged out.', 'INFO');
```

Once you have created your event and listener classes, you need to tell the framework that the listener should be executed when the event is dispatched. This is done in the `app/Providers/EventServiceProvider.php` file.

The `$listen` property is an array where the keys are the event classes and the values are arrays of listener classes that should handle them.

```php
// in app/Providers/EventServiceProvider.php
use App\Events\UserRegistered;
use App\Listeners\SendWelcomeEmail;

class EventServiceProvider
{
    protected array $listen = [
        // When the UserRegistered event is dispatched...
        UserRegistered::class => [
            // ...execute the SendWelcomeEmail listener.
            SendWelcomeEmail::class,
        ],
    ];
    // ...
}
```

The final step is to dispatch the event from your controller (or any other class). You can do this by injecting the `Rhapsody\Core\Events\EventDispatcher` into your controller's constructor and calling its `dispatch()` method.

```php
// in app/Controllers/AuthController.php
use App\Events\UserRegistered;
use Rhapsody\Core\Events\EventDispatcher;

class AuthController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(
        protected EventDispatcher $dispatcher,
        // ... other dependencies
    ) {
        // ...
    }

    public function register(Request $request): Response
    {
        // ... validation and user creation logic ...

        // After the user is saved, dispatch the event
        $this->dispatcher->dispatch(new UserRegistered($user));

        // ... redirect the user ...
    }
}
```

Now, your controller's only responsibility is to handle the registration. It no longer knows anything about sending emails. You can easily add more listeners (e.g., `LogUserRegistration`, `UpdateUserStats`) to the `EventServiceProvider` in the future without ever having to change your `AuthController` again.

Besides the `EventServiceProvider`, you can register a listener at any time with `listen()`. It accepts a class name or a callable, and anything else is a `TypeError`:

```php
$dispatcher->listen(UserRegistered::class, SendWelcomeEmail::class);

$dispatcher->listen(UserRegistered::class, function (UserRegistered $event): void {
    // runs after the listeners registered before it
});
```

A subscriber is a class that registers several listeners in one place. Give it a `subscribe()` method that receives the dispatcher, then hand the object to `$dispatcher->subscribe()`, which calls that method for you. An object without a `subscribe()` method is ignored.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\EventDispatcherInterface;

class UserEventSubscriber
{
    public function subscribe(EventDispatcherInterface $dispatcher): void
    {
        $dispatcher->listen(UserRegistered::class, SendWelcomeEmail::class);
        $dispatcher->listen(UserLoggedIn::class, LogLogin::class);
    }
}

$dispatcher->subscribe(new UserEventSubscriber());
```

An event opts into this behavior by implementing `Rhapsody\Core\Events\StoppableEventInterface`, which requires a single method:

```php
interface StoppableEventInterface
{
    public function isPropagationStopped(): bool;
}
```

Inside `EventDispatcher::dispatch()`, after every listener runs, the dispatcher asks two questions: does this event implement `StoppableEventInterface`, and if so, is `isPropagationStopped()` now `true`? If the answer to both is yes, the dispatcher stops calling any remaining listeners and hands the event straight back to whoever called `dispatch()`.

An event that is already stopped when it is dispatched reaches no listeners at all.

Important: this check only ever applies to events that implement the interface. `UserRegistered` and every other ordinary event you create do not implement it, so they are completely unaffected by any of this — every listener always runs for them, in the order they were registered, exactly as described in the sections above.

`RouteNotFound` is a good concrete example to study, and you can use it directly rather than building your own. It carries the unmatched URI and HTTP method, plus a place for a listener to put a response:

```php
class RouteNotFound extends Event implements StoppableEventInterface
{
    private ?Response $response = null;
    private bool $propagationStopped = false;

    public function __construct(
        public readonly string $uri,
        public readonly string $method,
    ) {}

    // A listener calls this when it has resolved the URI to something real.
    public function setResponse(Response $response): void
    {
        $this->response = $response;
        $this->propagationStopped = true; // setting a response always stops propagation
    }

    public function getResponse(): ?Response
    {
        return $this->response;
    }

    public function isPropagationStopped(): bool
    {
        return $this->propagationStopped;
    }
}
```

Notice that `setResponse()` is the only way to mark the event as stopped. You can't accidentally stop propagation without also providing a response, and you can't end up with two different responses from two different listeners — whichever listener calls `setResponse()` first wins, and no listener registered after it gets a turn.

A listener for a stoppable event is written exactly like any other listener (see 1. Creating Events and Listeners above). The only difference is in what it does inside `handle()`: if it finds a match, it calls `setResponse()`; if it doesn't, it simply returns and does nothing, leaving the event unstopped so the next listener — or the framework's normal fallback behavior — gets a chance.

```php
namespace App\Listeners;

use Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;

class RedirectLegacyUrls
{
    public function handle(RouteNotFound $event): void
    {
        // Look up $event->uri in your own table of old-slug -> new-slug redirects.
        $newPath = $this->findRedirectFor($event->uri);

        if ($newPath === null) {
            return; // no match — let the next listener try, or fall through to the 404
        }

        $response = new Response();
        $response->setStatusCode(301);
        $response->setHeader('Location', $newPath);

        $event->setResponse($response);
    }

    private function findRedirectFor(string $uri): ?string
    {
        // your own lookup logic here
        return null;
    }
}
```

You register this listener for `RouteNotFound` the same way you'd register any other listener — see 2. Registering Listeners above. If you're writing this inside a marketplace module rather than the main application, remember that a module also needs to declare `Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound` in its `events.listen.listen` permission list in `module.json` before it's allowed to listen for the event at all — a module that omits this will get a `ModulePermissionException` instead.

In your controller, build an uploader from the current `Request`, describe what you accept, and call `store()` with the name of the form field:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\FileUploader;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;

public function upload(Request $request): Response
{
    $result = new FileUploader($request)
        ->images()
        ->maxSize('5MB')
        ->store('documents');

    return $this->json($result->toArray(), $result->ok() ? 200 : 422);
}
```

Files are saved to `storage/uploads/` by default (created on first use). Each file is renamed to a random name, and its extension is derived from the detected MIME type, never from the client's filename.

`store()` returns an immutable `UploadResult`:

- `$result->ok()` / `$result->failed()` – true when every file was stored and nothing went wrong.
- `$result->files` – list of `UploadedFile` objects ( `originalName`, `filename`, `path`, `size`, `mimeType`).
- `$result->first()` – the first stored file, handy for single-file fields.
- `$result->errors` – messages keyed by the client's original filename. Problems not tied to one file (nothing sent, request too large) are under `_request`.
- `$result->firstError()` – the first error message, or `null`.
- `$result->toArray()` – a JSON-ready payload that never includes absolute server paths.

When some files succeed and others fail, the successful files stay stored and `ok()` returns false. Use the `path` property of an `UploadedFile` when you need its location on the server:

```php
$result = new FileUploader($request)->images()->maxSize('2MB')->store('avatar');

if ($result->failed()) {
    return $this->json(['error' => $result->firstError()], 422);
}

$avatar = $result->first();   // UploadedFile
$user->setAvatar($avatar->filename);
```

The JSON returned by `toArray()` looks like this:

```json
{
    "success": false,
    "files": [
        { "original_name": "cat.png", "filename": "9f2c…e1.png", "size": 48213, "mime_type": "image/png" }
    ],
    "errors": {
        "notes.exe": ["Files of type application/x-dosexec are not allowed."]
    }
}
```

Events bubble from the form, so you can react to uploads without touching the script:

- `rhapsody:upload:start` – `detail.files`
- `rhapsody:upload:success` – `detail.file`, `detail.response`
- `rhapsody:upload:error` – `detail.file`, `detail.message`, `detail.response`
- `rhapsody:upload:complete` – `detail.results`

```javascript
document.querySelector('form[data-rhapsody-upload]')
    .addEventListener('rhapsody:upload:success', (e) => {
        console.log('Stored:', e.detail.response.files);
    });
```

Use this function in your controllers whenever you need to redirect a request. It takes two arguments:

$url

and

$redirectCode

(optional). The redirect code defaults to `302` if it isn't set.

```php
// In your controller file

public function submitForm() {
    //--- if form posts successfully, redirect user to some other page ---//
    return redirect('/dashboard');
}

public function anotherExample() {
    //--- We want to set something other than 302 ---//
    $redirectCode = 308;
    return redirect('/dashboard', $redirectCode);
}
```

A few things to keep in mind:

- Return the result. `redirect()` returns a `RedirectResponse` object. The redirect is sent when that response is returned from your controller.
- Subdirectory installs are handled for you. For paths like `/dashboard`, Rhapsody prepends the `APP_BASE_URL` value from your `.env` file automatically. Never include it yourself. The same code works whether your site lives at the domain root or in a subdirectory.
- Absolute URLs are left alone. `redirect('https://example.com/thanks')` is sent exactly as written.
- The status code must be a 3xx code. Anything outside 300–399 throws an `InvalidArgumentException`. The most common choices are `301` (permanent), `302` (temporary, the default), `303` (see other, ideal after a form POST), `307` (temporary, keeps the request method) and `308` (permanent, keeps the request method).
- Flash a message with the redirect by chaining `with()`. See flash below for reading it in your templates.

```php
return redirect('/cart/out-of-stock')->with('error', 'This item is out of stock.');
```

The current environment, from `APP_ENV`: `development` or `production` (the default if unset). Handy for showing things only while developing.

```twig
{% if app_env == 'development' %}
    Development mode
{% endif %}
```

Information about the current visitor's login state, with two properties:

- `auth.check`: `true` if a user is logged in, otherwise `false`.
- `auth.user`: the logged-in user's record, or `null` for guests.

```twig
{% if auth.check %}
    Welcome back, {{ auth.user.name }}!
    Log out
{% else %}
    Log in
{% endif %}
```

`auth` is lazy: the user is only loaded from the database when you actually use `auth.user`, so pages that only call `auth.check` cost nothing extra. The fields available on `auth.user` depend on what your `User` model returns. Each use of `auth.user` looks the user up again, so if you need it several times on one page, store it once:

```twig
{% set user = auth.user %}
{{ user.name }} · {{ user.email }}
```

Gives templates access to one-time flash messages, such as those set with `redirect()->with()`. Read a message by the key you used when setting it.

```php
// Controller
return redirect('/dashboard')->with('success', 'Your profile was updated.');
```

```twig
{# Template #}
{% if flash.success %}
    {{ flash.success }}
{% endif %}
```

Like `auth`, flash messages are read on demand, so there is no cost when a page doesn't use them.

This Twig function is used in conjunction with the image manipulation library. It builds URLs and links to images, and provides resizing, signed URLs, caching and other utilities. This is the recommended way to serve your graphic files, but requires a couple more steps than just pasting a link.

If you don't need any of the advanced features of the image library and just want to drop in a quick link, either use the Path helper in your controllers, or `{{ base_url }}` in your view files directly to link to your images.

```php
// In your controller. Make sure to import Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path
$imgPath = Path::public('images/icons');
$img = 'user.jpg';
// Builds the full image url that you can then pass to your view
$imgUrl = $imgPath . $img;
```

```twig
// Or, link to the same image directly in your view file
<img src="{{ base_url }}/public/images/icons/user.jpg">
```

A wildcard route captures all image requests. It is already registered in the skeleton's `routes/web.php` file and should not be removed:

```php
// in routes/web.php
use App\Controllers\ImageController;

Router::get('/img/{path}', [ImageController::class, 'show']);
```

This route directs any URL starting with `/img/` to the `ImageController`.

The source directory is set in the `show()` method of `app/Controllers/ImageController.php`, on the `source` line of the Glide server configuration. To serve images from somewhere else, such as a theme folder, change that path:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;

$server = ServerFactory::create([
    // Default: Path::public('images')
    'source'         => Path::public('themes/default/images'),
    'cache'          => $cachePath,
    'max_image_size' => self::MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS,
]);
```

`Path` is a small helper in `Rhapsody\Core\Helpers` that returns absolute filesystem paths. `Path::public('themes/default/images')` resolves to `public/themes/default/images` inside your project, using the correct directory separator for your operating system. Use it (or the related `Path::root()`, `Path::storage()`, and `Path::resources()` methods) instead of hard-coding absolute paths, so the controller works on any server.

A few things to keep in mind when you change the directory:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Kernel;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;

$kernel = new Kernel($container, $config);
$request = new Request();

try {
    $response = $kernel->handle($request);
    $response = $kernel->terminate($request, $response);
    $response->send();
} catch (HttpException $e) {
    // ErrorHandler handles this globally
    throw $e;
}
```

The pure nature of `Kernel::handle()` makes it easy to test:

```php
$kernel = new Kernel($container, $config);
$request = new Request();

$response = $kernel->handle($request);
$this->assertEquals(200, $response->getStatusCode());
```

```php
// config.php
'lazy' => [
    'enabled' => filter_var($_ENV['LAZY_LOADING_ENABLED'] ?? true, FILTER_VALIDATE_BOOLEAN),
    // ...
]
```

You can disable lazy loading entirely by setting `LAZY_LOADING_ENABLED=false` in your `.env` file.

Suppose you have a heavy service `ReportGenerator` that generates complex PDF reports. It is rarely used – only on a specific admin page.

```php
// Service with heavy constructor
class ReportGenerator
{
    public function __construct(
        private PdfEngine $engine,
        private Database $db,
        private Logger $logger
    ) {
        // This can be slow: connecting to PDF service, loading fonts, etc.
        $this->engine->loadFonts();
        $this->logger->info('ReportGenerator initialized');
    }

    public function generate(): string { ... }
}
```

Without lazy loading, `ReportGenerator` would be built on every request where it is injected (even if not used). With lazy loading, the service is only created when `generate()` is called.

```php
// Controller that uses the service only in one method
class AdminController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(
        private ReportGenerator $reportGenerator // <- this is a proxy, not the real object
    ) {}

    public function dashboard(Request $request): Response
    {
        // If this method returns early, the report generator is never instantiated
        if (! $request->hasParam('generate')) {
            return $this->view('admin/dashboard');
        }

        // Only now does the real ReportGenerator get built
        $pdf = $this->reportGenerator->generate();
        return $this->fileResponse($pdf);
    }
}
```

The first request (without `generate`) will not construct `ReportGenerator`, saving time and resources. Only when the method is actually called does the proxy trigger initialisation.

To use the logger, you first need to tell the framework where your log files are located. This is done in the `config.php` file within the `logging` array. You can define paths for different log types, such as PHP and Apache errors.

```php
// in config.php
'logging' => [
    // Example for MAMP on macOS
    'php_error_log_path' => '/Applications/MAMP/logs/php_error.log',
    'apache_error_log_path' => '/Applications/MAMP/logs/apache_error.log',
    // You can also define a custom log path
    'application_log_path' => dirname(__DIR__) . '/storage/logs/app.log',
],
```

The logger will automatically create the directory if it does not exist when you write to it.

The `log()` method is used to write messages. It automatically adds a timestamp and a log level to each entry.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Logger;

// Get the path from your config
$logPath = $config['logging']['application_log_path'];
$logger = new Logger($logPath);

// Write an informational message
$logger->log('User successfully updated their profile.', 'INFO');

// Write an error message
try {
    // ... some operation that might fail ...
} catch (\Exception $e) {
    $logger->log('Failed to process payment: ' . $e->getMessage(), 'ERROR');
}
```

This will produce entries in your `storage/logs/app.log` file like this:

```bash
[2025-10-02 22:30:00] INFO: User successfully updated their profile.
[2025-10-02 22:30:01] ERROR: Failed to process payment: Connection timed out.
```

The `read()` method allows you to retrieve the last N lines of a log file. This is particularly useful for displaying recent activity or errors, as seen in the developer toolbar.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Logger;

$phpErrorLog = new Logger($config['logging']['php_error_log_path']);

// Read the last 50 lines of the PHP error log
$recentErrors = $phpErrorLog->read(50);

echo "<pre>{$recentErrors}</pre>";
```

The easiest way to send mail is to inject the `Rhapsody\Core\Mailer` service into your controller's constructor via dependency injection. Then, call the `send()` method.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Mailer;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;

class ContactController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(protected Mailer $mailer) {}

    public function submit(Request $request): Response
    {
        // ... validation ...
        try {
            $this->mailer->send('admin@example.com', 'New Contact', 'Message');
            return redirect('/contact')->with('success', 'Message sent!');
        } catch (\Exception $e) {
            // Log error
            return redirect('/contact')->with('error', 'Could not send message.');
        }
    }
}
```

Important: If your `.env` file is missing `MAIL_HOST` (or it is empty), the `Mailer` service will throw a `RuntimeException` when you try to send an email. Always configure your mail settings before using the mailer in production.

```php
// config/middleware.php
return [
    // Route‑specific middleware (key => class)
    'map' => [
        'auth'    => \App\Middleware\AuthMiddleware::class,
        'guest'   => \App\Middleware\GuestMiddleware::class,
        // Add your own:
        // 'admin' => \App\Middleware\AdminMiddleware::class,
    ],
    // Global middleware (runs on every matched request)
    'global' => [
        \App\Middleware\VerifyCsrfTokenMiddleware::class,
    ],
];
```

Chain the `middleware()` method to your route definition using the keys defined in `middleware.php`.

```php
// Only for authenticated users
Router::get('/dashboard', [PageController::class, 'dashboard'])->middleware('auth');

// Only for guests
Router::get('/login', [AuthController::class, 'showLoginForm'])->middleware('guest');
```

1. Create a class in `app/Middleware` extending `App\Middleware\Middleware`.
2. Implement the `handle(Request $request): ?Response` method.
3. Add an entry to the `'map'` array in `config/middleware.php`.

```php
// Example AdminMiddleware
namespace App\Middleware;

use Rhapsody\Core\Request;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;
use Rhapsody\Core\Session;

class AdminMiddleware extends Middleware
{
    public function handle(Request $request): ?Response
    {
        if (Session::get('user_role') !== 'admin') {
            return redirect('/');
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

Then register it in `config/middleware.php`:

```php
'map' => [
    // ...
    'admin' => \App\Middleware\AdminMiddleware::class,
],
```

A model typically represents one database table. For example, a `User` model would handle all operations for the `users` table.

```php
<?php

namespace App\Models;

use Rhapsody\Core\BaseModel;
use PDO;

class User extends BaseModel
{
    /**
     * Fetches a single user from the database by their ID.
     */
    public function findById(string $id): array|false
    {
        // Use prepare() for security against SQL injection
        $stmt = $this->db->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE user_id = :id");

        // Bind the parameter
        $stmt->bindParam(':id', $id, PDO::PARAM_STR);

        // Execute and fetch the result
        $stmt->execute();
        return $stmt->fetch();
    }

    /**
     * Fetches all users.
     */
    public function findAll(): array
    {
        $stmt = $this->db->query("SELECT user_id, name, email FROM users ORDER BY name");
        return $stmt->fetchAll();
    }
}
```

This is critical. Whenever you are using user-provided data (like a route parameter or form input) in a query, you must use prepared statements to prevent SQL injection.

The correct workflow is to use `prepare()`, then `bindParam()` or pass an array to `execute()`. Never concatenate variables directly into a SQL string.

```php
// GOOD: Secure and correct
$stmt = $this->db->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = :email");
$stmt->execute([':email' => $email]);
$user = $stmt->fetch();

// BAD: Insecure and will lead to vulnerabilities
$user = $this->db->query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '$email'");
```

PSR-4 is the standard PHP uses to map a class's namespace onto a file path, so that PHP can find and load a class file automatically the first time that class is used — you never write a manual `require` or `include` for your own classes. In a module's `composer.json`, this line:

```json
"autoload": {
    "psr-4": { "Acme\\WelcomeBonus\\": "src/" }
}
```

means "any class under the `Acme\WelcomeBonus` namespace lives in the `src/` folder, in a matching path." So `Acme\WelcomeBonus\ModuleProvider` must live at `src/ModuleProvider.php`, and `Acme\WelcomeBonus\Support\Formatter` (if you had one) would live at `src/Support/Formatter.php`. Get this mapping wrong — wrong folder, wrong namespace, typo in either — and your `provider` class in `module.json` simply won't be found. This is, in practice, one of the most common first-time mistakes; see Troubleshooting.

```php
<?php
namespace Acme\WelcomeBonus;

use Rhapsody\Core\Events\UserRegistered;
use Rhapsody\Core\Modules\Contracts\ModuleServiceProviderInterface;
use Rhapsody\Core\Modules\ModuleContext;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;

class ModuleProvider implements ModuleServiceProviderInterface
{
    public function boot(ModuleContext $context): void
    {
        // Listen for user registration events.
        $context->events()->listen(UserRegistered::class, function (UserRegistered $event) use ($context) {
            $amount = $context->settings()->get('bonus_amount', 10);
            $context->storage()->put(
                'bonus-log.txt',
                sprintf("[%s] granted $%s to user #%s\n", date('c'), $amount, $event->user->getUserId())
            );
        });

        // Register a route: mounted at /welcome-bonus/balance
        $context->routes()->get('/balance', function () use ($context) {
            $amount = $context->settings()->get('bonus_amount', 10);
            return (new Response())->setContent("Current welcome bonus: \${$amount}");
        });
    }

    public function install(ModuleContext $context): void
    {
        // Set default settings when the module is first activated.
        $context->settings()->set('bonus_amount', 10);
    }

    public function uninstall(ModuleContext $context): void
    {
        // Clean up whatever install() created.
        $context->storage()->delete('bonus-log.txt');
    }
}
```

Walking through `boot()` line by line, for anyone who wants the full mental model:

*`RouteNotFound` is not like the other three. `UserRegistered` and the two payment events are plain notifications — nothing your listener does can change what core does next. `RouteNotFound` is a stoppable event: if your listener calls `$event->setResponse(...)` — for example, after resolving the requested URI against your module's own table of old-slug-to-new-slug redirects — the router returns that response directly instead of showing a 404, and no other `RouteNotFound` listener runs. If your listener finds nothing, just return without calling `setResponse()` and the normal 404 proceeds untouched. See Stoppable Events for the full mechanism behind this, and Routing for how the router uses it.

Declare it in your manifest — remember, no wildcards, you list every event class you want by its full name:

```json
"permissions": {
    "events.listen": {
        "listen": [
            "Rhapsody\\Core\\Events\\UserRegistered",
            "Rhapsody\\Core\\Events\\PaymentFailedEvent"
        ]
    }
}
```

Then, in your provider:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Events\UserRegistered;
use Rhapsody\Core\Events\PaymentFailedEvent;

public function boot(ModuleContext $context): void
{
    $context->events()->listen(UserRegistered::class, function (UserRegistered $event) {
        // $event->user is the newly registered User entity
        error_log("Welcome, user #{$event->user->getUserId()}!");
    });

    $context->events()->listen(PaymentFailedEvent::class, function (PaymentFailedEvent $event) {
        // e.g. notify an admin, log to a monitoring service
        error_log("Payment failed: {$event->message}");
    });
}
```

Under the hood, what actually happens when you call `listen()`:

Lets you register HTTP routes: `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`. There are two distinct ways to do this, and the difference matters.

**Namespaced routes (the common case)**

```php
$context->routes()->get('/balance', function () {
    return (new \Rhapsody\Core\Response())->setContent('42');
});
```

This is not mounted at `/balance`. It's automatically mounted at `/{prefix}/balance`, where `{prefix}` is either your module's own slug (its `name` field with the slash turned into a dash — e.g. `acme/welcome-bonus` becomes `acme-welcome-bonus`) or, if you set one, the custom `prefix` from your manifest:

```json
"routes.register": { "prefix": "welcome-bonus" }
```

With that manifest, `$context->routes()->get('/balance', ...)` ends up live at exactly `/welcome-bonus/balance`. This namespacing is automatic and mandatory for this method — it's what guarantees your module's routes can never collide with another module's routes, or with an app route, no matter what path string you pass in.

**Root-level routes (the exception)**

Sometimes a route genuinely needs to live at the true root of the site — a `sitemap.xml`, a `robots.txt`, a fixed webhook URL a third-party service expects at an exact path. For that, there's a second method, `root()`, which bypasses the prefix entirely — but only for paths you explicitly whitelist:

```json
"routes.register": {
    "prefix": "seo-toolkit",
    "paths": ["/sitemap.xml", "/robots.txt"]
}
```

```php
$context->routes()->root('GET', '/sitemap.xml', function () {
    // build and return the sitemap
});
$context->routes()->root('GET', '/robots.txt', function () {
    // build and return robots.txt
});
```

Try to call `root()` with a path that isn't in your `paths` whitelist and it throws: `Module "your-slug" tried to register root-level route "/some/path", which isn't in its declared routes.register.paths whitelist`. This is the same "explicit list, no wildcard" pattern as `events.listen` — you can have exactly the root path you need, and nothing more.

**Advanced: reading the live route table**

There's a third thing `routes.register` unlocks, gated by the same permission: read-only access to every route already registered on the site (app routes, core routes, and any module routes booted before yours), via `$context->routes()->registered()`. This is genuinely useful — for example, the shipped `acme/seo-toolkit` module builds `sitemap.xml` by reading the real route table instead of maintaining a hand-written URL list:

```php
foreach ($context->routes()->registered() as $route) {
    // $route is a RegisteredRoute: ->method, ->path, ->name, ->middleware, ->controller
    if ($route->method !== 'GET') {
        continue; // nothing to crawl
    }
    if ($route->hasParams()) {
        continue; // can't enumerate a concrete URL for e.g. /docs/{slug}
    }
    // ...more filtering, then build a <url> entry from $route->path
}
```

Read the full thing at `vendor/arout/rhapsody-core/examples/acme-seo-toolkit/src/ModuleProvider.php` — it's a complete, working example of every filtering decision you'd realistically need to make (excluding non-public routes, excluding routes behind certain middleware, excluding routes owned by a specific controller class).

Rhapsody's themes are rendered with Twig, a templating language — the `.twig` files in `views/themes/`. These two permissions let a module add its own logic that theme authors can call from inside a template.

`twig.functions` is the simpler of the two — a single callable exposed as a function:

```php
$context->twig()->addFunction('formatBonus', function ($amount) {
    return '$' . number_format($amount, 2);
});
```

In a template, this is not called as `formatBonus(10)`. Every function name you register is automatically prefixed `mod_{your-slug}_`, so it's callable as:

```twig
{{ mod_welcome_bonus_formatBonus(10) }}
{# outputs: $10.00 #}
```

This prefixing is enforced, not optional — it's what guarantees two different modules (or a module and core itself) can never silently overwrite each other's Twig functions just because they both picked the name `format`.

`twig.extensions` is for the more advanced case where you have a full `Twig\Extension\ExtensionInterface` class (multiple functions/filters/tests bundled together) rather than a single callable:

```php
$context->twig()->addExtension(new MyModuleTwigExtension());
```

Declare only the one(s) you actually use — most modules only need `twig.functions`, if they need Twig integration at all.

Grants read access to every table in the application's database, and write access (including creating your own tables) to a table namespace reserved for your module alone — every table starting with `mod_{your-slug}_`. That prefix is not something you declare: it's derived automatically from your manifest's own `name` field, the exact same way `slug()` already is. There's no permissions-block field for it, on purpose — if a module could simply declare its own table prefix, it could declare `"tablePrefix": "users"` and write to a table it has no business touching. Deriving it from identity that's already fixed at manifest-parse time closes that off entirely.

Declare it in `module.json` with no extra configuration, same as `storage.access`:

```json
{
    "permissions": {
        "database.access": {}
    }
}
```

The permission gates reads too — this is the one place that's easy to get backwards relative to `settings.manage`, where reads are free and only writes require the key. Here, `query()`, `select()`, `find()`, and `findBy()` all check `can('database.access')` exactly like the write methods do. What's asymmetric isn't whether the permission is required, but which tables a write is allowed to touch.

`DatabaseFacade` offers two ways in — plain SQL, or Doctrine — and the same ownership rule applies to both:

```php
public function recordClick(ModuleContext $context, int $itemId): void
{
    $context->database()->insert('mod_acme_click_tracker_clicks', [
        'item_id'    => $itemId,
        'clicked_at' => date('Y-m-d H:i:s'),
    ]);
}

public function topItems(ModuleContext $context): array
{
    // Reading your own table works the same as reading any other —
    // select()/query() aren't scoped by ownership, only writes are.
    return $context->database()->select(
        'mod_acme_click_tracker_clicks',
        [],
        ['item_id', 'COUNT(*) as total']
    );
}
```

Doctrine example — the same module, using an entity instead of raw SQL:

```php
