Notice everything here is `root()`-only — no namespaced prefix routes at all, because a sitemap only makes sense at the true site root, never under a module-specific path. Notice too that it doesn't declare `storage.access` or any `twig.*` permission, because it genuinely doesn't need them — it generates its output fresh on every request straight from the route table, rather than caching to a file. This is worth internalizing as a habit: only ask for the permissions your module actually uses.

The core of its `boot()` method — reading the live route table and filtering it down to what's actually safe to publish:

```php
public function boot(ModuleContext $context): void
{
    $context->routes()->root('GET', '/sitemap.xml', fn () => $this->sitemapResponse($context));
    $context->routes()->root('GET', '/robots.txt', fn () => $this->robotsResponse($context));
}

private function sitemapResponse(ModuleContext $context): Response
{
    $excludedPrefixes    = $this->csv($context->settings()->get('excluded_prefixes', ''));
    $excludedControllers = $this->csv($context->settings()->get('excluded_controllers', ''));
    $excludedMiddleware  = $this->csv($context->settings()->get('excluded_middleware', 'auth'));

    $urls = [];
    foreach ($context->routes()->registered() as $route) {
        if ($route->method !== 'GET') continue;
        if ($route->hasParams()) continue;                                          // e.g. /docs/{slug}
        if (array_intersect($route->middleware, $excludedMiddleware)) continue;      // e.g. behind "auth"
        if ($this->startsWithAny($route->path, $excludedPrefixes)) continue;         // e.g. /payment/*
        if ($route->controller && in_array($route->controller, $excludedControllers, true)) continue;

        $urls[$route->path] = true; // dedupe
    }

    // ...build and return the XML from array_keys($urls)
}
```

Your model needs two methods: one to count total records, and one to fetch a specific slice using `LIMIT` and `OFFSET`.

```php
// app/Models/User.php
public function countAll(): int
{
    $stmt = $this->db->query("SELECT COUNT(user_id) FROM users");
    return (int) $stmt->fetchColumn();
}

public function findPaginated(int $limit, int $offset): array
{
    $sql = "SELECT ... FROM users ... LIMIT :limit OFFSET :offset";
    $stmt = $this->db->prepare($sql);
    $stmt->bindParam(':limit', $limit, PDO::PARAM_INT);
    $stmt->bindParam(':offset', $offset, PDO::PARAM_INT);
    $stmt->execute();
    return $stmt->fetchAll();
}
```

Instantiate the `Pagination` class with the total number of items, the desired limit, and the current page.

```php
// app/Controllers/PageController.php
use Rhapsody\Core\Pagination;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;

public function showUsers(Request $request): Response
{
    $usersPerPage = (int) $request->getQueryParam('limit', 10);
    $currentPage  = (int) $request->getQueryParam('page', 1);

    $totalUsers = $this->userModel->countAll();

    $pagination = new Pagination($totalUsers, $usersPerPage, $currentPage);

    // Optional: add custom CSS classes to the rendered navigation
    $pagination->setCssClasses([
        'container' => 'pagination-container',
        'list'      => 'pagination-list',
        'item'      => 'pagination-item',
        'active'    => 'is-active',
        'disabled'  => 'is-disabled',
        'link'      => 'pagination-link',
    ]);

    $users = $this->userModel->findPaginated(
        $pagination->getLimit(),
        $pagination->getOffset()
    );

    return $this->view('home/users.twig', [
        'users'      => $users,
        'pagination' => $pagination,
    ]);
}
```

By default, page links are relative (`?page=2`), resolving against whatever URL the current page was requested on. If you're rendering pagination somewhere that URL isn't the right target — for example, a listing reachable from more than one route — use `setBaseUrl()` to make links absolute to a specific path instead:

```php
$pagination->setBaseUrl('/users/list');
// Links become "/users/list?page=2" instead of "?page=2"
```

By default, `render()` shows every page number — unchanged from the original behavior. For large page counts, pass `maxLinks` (either via `setMaxLinks()` as a standing default, or as a one-off `render()` option) to show a window of pages centered on the current one, with First/Last links and an ellipsis appearing automatically whenever page 1 or the last page falls outside that window:

```php
// Standing default — every render() call after this uses a 7-page window
$pagination->setMaxLinks(7);

// One-off override for a single render() call
echo $pagination->render(['maxLinks' => 7]);
// e.g. with 20 total pages, on page 10:
// Previous  First … 8 9 [10] 11 12 … Last  Next
```

Enable a `<select>` that lets visitors jump straight to any page, rendered immediately after the nav:

```php
$pagination->setShowJumpMenu(true);
echo $pagination->render();
```

It works with no JavaScript — a Go button submits a standard GET form that preserves every other current query parameter as hidden inputs — and progressively enhances to jump immediately on `<select>` change when JavaScript is available. Only simple (string or numeric) query parameters are carried over; nested array parameters such as `?filter[]=x` are skipped because they can't be represented by a single hidden input. To place the menu somewhere other than right after the nav, call `renderJumpMenu()` on its own instead of passing `showJumpMenu` to `render()`:

```twig
{{ pagination.render()|raw }}
<div class="mt-4">
    {{ pagination.renderJumpMenu()|raw }}
</div>
```

`getDataAttributes()` returns an associative array of `data-*` attributes describing the current pagination state, for a JavaScript-driven pagination widget to read:

```php
$pagination->getDataAttributes();
// [
//     'data-pagination'   => '',
//     'data-current-page' => 3,
//     'data-total-pages'  => 12,
//     'data-per-page'     => 10,
//     'data-total-items'  => 117,
// ]
```

Instead of the built-in markup, hand off rendering to your own Twig template with `renderTemplate()`. The `Pagination` instance is passed into the template as `pagination`, giving you direct access to `pagination.pageRange`, `pagination.previousPage`, `pagination.nextPage`, and `pagination.url(n)` — set `setMaxLinks()` beforehand if you want `pagination.pageRange` to reflect a window rather than every page:

```php
$pagination->setMaxLinks(5);
echo $pagination->renderTemplate('@app/components/pagination.twig');
```

```twig
<nav>
    {{# pagination.previousPage %} <a href="{{ pagination.url(pagination.previousPage) }}">←</a> {{% endif %}

    {{% for page in pagination.pageRange %}
        <a href="{{ pagination.url(page) }}">{{ page }}</a>
    {{% endfor %}

    {{# pagination.nextPage %} <a href="{{ pagination.url(pagination.nextPage) }}">→</a> {{% endif %}
</nav>
```

Note: `renderTemplate()` resolves the framework's Twig environment via `Container::getInstance()`, so it requires a bootstrapped container — see the Service Container documentation. Plain `render()` has no such requirement and works anywhere, including isolated unit tests.

```twig
{% include "@core/partials/pagination-ajax.twig" %}
```

