`Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path` is a small static helper that returns absolute filesystem paths to the important directories of your application. Whenever your code needs to read, write, include, or check a file, build the location with `Path` instead of hand-written relative paths. It is dependency-free, requires no setup, and costs next to nothing at runtime.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;

$logFile = Path::logs('import.log');
// /var/www/mysite/storage/logs/import.log
```

Paths, not URLs: `Path` gives you locations on the server's disk. It never returns a web address. See Paths vs URLs below.

Pass the rest of the location as the argument. You can use forward slashes everywhere, even on Windows:

```php
Path::app('Controllers/HomeController.php');
Path::config('mail.php');
Path::public('uploads/avatars');
Path::views('emails/welcome.twig');
Path::root('.env');
```

```php
$settings = require Path::config('custom-settings.php');
```

```php
$file = Path::logs('payments.log');

file_put_contents($file, date('c') . " Payment received\n", FILE_APPEND | LOCK_EX);
```

```php
$directory = Path::public('uploads/avatars');

if (!is_dir($directory)) {
    mkdir($directory, 0755, true);
}

move_uploaded_file($tmpName, $directory . DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR . $safeFilename);
```

For a complete upload workflow with validation, see the File Uploader documentation.

```php
$theme = $_ENV['APP_THEME'] ?? 'default';

$imageDirectory = Path::public('themes/' . $theme . '/images');
```

This is also how you would change where the image processor looks for source images. See Image Processing.

```php
if (is_file(Path::storage('exports/report.csv'))) {
    // ...
}
```

This is the most common point of confusion. `Path` answers “where is this file on the server?”. A browser needs to be told “what address do I request?”.

```php
// Filesystem path – use for PHP file operations
Path::public('uploads/photo.jpg');
// C:\wamp64\www\mysite\public\uploads\photo.jpg   (Windows)
// /var/www/mysite/public/uploads/photo.jpg        (Linux)
```

```twig
<img src="{{ base_url }}/uploads/photo.jpg">
```

Rule of thumb: PHP file functions get a `Path`; HTML gets a URL. Never output a `Path` value into a page, as it would expose your server's directory structure.

`Path` is a path builder, not a sandbox. It does not strip `../` segments, so a value taken straight from a request could escape the folder you intended.

Danger: `Path::storage('uploads/' . $_GET['file'])` can be abused with `?file=../../.env`.

Reduce user input to a bare filename with `basename()`, or verify the resolved location stays inside the expected folder:

```php
$base = realpath(Path::storage('uploads'));
$file = realpath(Path::storage('uploads/' . $name));

if ($base === false || $file === false || !str_starts_with($file, $base . DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR)) {
    // Reject the request
}
```

Need a shortcut for a folder your app uses constantly? Extend `Path` in your application and add a method. All of the inherited methods keep working.

```php
namespace App\Helpers;

use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;

class AppPath extends Path
{
    public static function uploads(string $path = ''): string
    {
        return self::storage('uploads/' . ltrim($path, '/\\'));
    }
}

// Usage
AppPath::uploads('invoices/2026-001.pdf');
```

Remember the Golden Rule: never edit files in `/vendor`. Extending the class in your own `app/` directory keeps your additions safe during framework updates.

This method simplifies the process of getting and setting cache items:

1. It checks if a value exists in the cache for a given key.
2. If it exists and is not expired, it returns the cached value.
3. If it does not exist, it executes the provided function, saves its return value to the cache for a specified number of minutes, and then returns that value.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Cache;
use App\Models\Post;

class PostController extends BaseController
{
    // 1. Inject the Cache service in the constructor
    public function __construct(
        protected Post $postModel,
        protected Cache $cache,
        //... other dependencies
    ) {
        //...
    }

    public function index()
    {
        // 2. Use the injected instance to call remember()
        $posts = $this->cache->remember('posts.all', 60, function () {
            // This expensive query only runs when the cache is empty or expired
            return $this->postModel->findAll();
        });

        return $this->view('posts/index.twig', ['posts' => $posts]);
    }
}
```

This method works seamlessly regardless of which cache driver is active.

- Transactional & Marketing Emails / SMS: Sending password resets, welcome emails, or bulk notifications via third-party providers (like Mailgun, SendGrid, or Twilio).
- File & Media Processing: Generating PDF reports, resizing uploaded profile images, converting video formats, or parsing large CSV/Excel imports.
- Third-Party API Integrations: Communicating with external webhooks, payment gateways (e.g., Stripe processing webhooks), or syncing data with external CRMs where network latency or API rate limits could slow down your app.
- Scheduled / Batch Operations: Running routine database cleanups, generating daily analytics summaries, or processing batch data updates overnight.

```php
// In a controller: queue the job and answer the visitor straight away
dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($user->id));

return $this->view('auth/registered.twig');
```

Queue or `defer()`? `defer()` runs a task right after the response, in the same process, and forgets it if the server stops. The queue stores the job first. Use `defer()` for quick best-effort work; use the queue for anything that must not be lost (payments, important emails, imports) or that needs retries.

1. Create the tables (once per project):

```bash
php rhapsody queue:install --migrate
```

This publishes a migration into `db/migrations` and runs it. Without `--migrate` it only publishes the file; run `php rhapsody migrate` yourself when you are ready.

2. Write a job:

```php
namespace App\Jobs;

use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\JobInterface;

final class SendWelcomeEmail implements JobInterface
{
    public function __construct(public readonly int $userId) {}

    public function handle(UserRepository $users, WelcomeMailer $mailer): void
    {
        $mailer->sendTo($users->find($this->userId));
    }
}
```

3. Dispatch it:

```php
dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($user->id));
```

4. Run a worker:

```bash
php rhapsody queue:work
```

Implement `FailableJobInterface` to be told when a job has used up all its attempts. It runs once, after the job has been moved to the failed jobs.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\FailableJobInterface;
use Throwable;

final class ChargeInvoice implements FailableJobInterface
{
    public function __construct(public readonly int $invoiceId) {}

    public function handle(Billing $billing): void
    {
        $billing->charge($this->invoiceId);
    }

    public function failed(Throwable $exception): void
    {
        error_log("Invoice {$this->invoiceId} could not be charged: " . $exception->getMessage());
    }
}
```

`failed()` is called directly, so it does not get container injection. If it throws, the error is logged and ignored.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\QueueManager;

// 1. The global helper
dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($id));
dispatch(new SendReminder($id), queue: 'emails', delay: 3600);

// 2. Inject the manager (easier to test and to swap)
public function __construct(private QueueManager $queue) {}

$jobId = $this->queue->dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($id));
$waiting = $this->queue->size('emails');
```

Argument | Meaning
`$job` | The job object to run.
`queue` | Name of the queue (1–100 characters). Default: `default`.
`delay` | Seconds to wait before the job may first run. Default: `0`.

Both return the new job's id. A negative delay or an invalid queue name throws `InvalidArgumentException`.

By default the queue uses the same database as the rest of your app. To point it somewhere else, or to change the retry window, bind your own `QueueInterface` in your project's `bootstrap.php`. The worker, `dispatch()` and every queue command pick it up automatically.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Bootstrap\ServiceRegistrar;
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\QueueInterface;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\DatabaseQueue;

return static function (ServiceRegistrar $services): void {
    $services->singleton(QueueInterface::class, fn () => new DatabaseQueue(
        static fn (): PDO => new PDO($dsn, $user, $password, [PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION]),
        retryAfter: 120,
    ));
};
```

The `jobs` and `failed_jobs` tables must exist in that database. `QueueInterface` is a small contract (`push`, `pop`, `release`, `delete`, `fail`, plus the failed-job methods), so a Redis or SQS driver can be dropped in the same way.

For single-page applications or pages entirely powered by React, use the `react()` method in your controller (in place of the normal

view()

call):

```php
// In a controller
class DashboardController extends BaseController
{
    public function index(): Response
    {
        return $this->react('Dashboard', [
            'user' => [
                'id' => 1,
                'name' => 'John Doe',
                'email' => 'john@example.com',
            ],
            'stats' => [
                'posts' => 42,
                'followers' => 128,
            ],
        ], [
            'title' => 'Dashboard',
            'description' => 'User dashboard with live statistics',
        ]);
    }
}
```

This renders a full HTML page with:

- Your component mounted at `#rhapsody-root`
- Props passed safely via a `<script type="application/json">` block
- CSRF token available as a `<meta>` tag
- Vite assets automatically injected (dev server or production manifest)

The component is resolved from `resources/js/components/{ComponentName}.jsx`. Nested paths are supported: `$this->react('Users/ProfileCard', $props)`.

For embedding React components within traditional Twig templates, use the `react_component()` function:

```twig
{# In your Twig template #}
{% extends "@core/layouts/main.twig" %}

{% block content %}
    <h1>Welcome to your dashboard</h1>

    <p>Here's your live statistics:</p>

    {{ react_component('UserStats', {
        userId: auth.user.id,
        period: 'monthly',
        stats: stats
    }) }}

    <p>And your recent activity:</p>

    {{ react_component('ActivityFeed', { limit: 10 }) }}
{% endblock %}
```

Each component gets its own mount point with isolated props. The JavaScript bridge automatically finds all `.rhapsody-island` elements and mounts the specified components.

The `resources/js/app.jsx` entry point contains the Rhapsody bridge that automatically mounts both full-page components and islands:

```javascript
// resources/js/app.jsx
import React from 'react';
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import App from './App.jsx';  // Full-page application

// Import your components
import UserStats from './components/UserStats.jsx';
import ActivityFeed from './components/ActivityFeed.jsx';

// --- 1. Full-Page React App ---
const root = document.getElementById('rhapsody-root');
if (root) {
    const componentName = root.getAttribute('data-component');
    const props = JSON.parse(
        document.getElementById('rhapsody-props').textContent
    );

    // Dynamic import based on component name
    // This keeps the bundle size small
    import(`./components/${componentName}.jsx`)
        .then(module => {
            const Component = module.default;
            const reactRoot = createRoot(root);
            reactRoot.render(<Component {...props} />);
        });
}

// --- 2. React Islands ---
document.querySelectorAll('.rhapsody-island').forEach(element => {
    const componentName = element.getAttribute('data-component');
    const propsElement = element.querySelector('.rhapsody-island-props');
    const props = JSON.parse(propsElement.textContent);

    import(`./components/${componentName}.jsx`)
        .then(module => {
            const Component = module.default;
            const reactRoot = createRoot(element);
            reactRoot.render(<Component {...props} />);
        });
});
```

The generated component stub looks like this:

```javascript
// resources/js/components/Dashboard.jsx
import React from 'react';

/**
 * Dashboard component
 *
 * Props:
 * @param {Object} user - Current user data
 * @param {Array} stats - User statistics
 */
export default function Dashboard({ user, stats }) {
    return (
        <div className="p-6">
            <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">Welcome, {user.name}!</h1>
            <p>You have {stats?.length || 0} items.</p>
        </div>
    );
}
```

The integration automatically injects the CSRF token as a `<meta>` tag:

```html
<meta name="csrf-token" content="your_csrf_token_here">
```

In React, you can read it for secure POST requests:

```javascript
// In your React component
const csrfToken = document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content;

const response = await fetch('/api/data', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        'X-CSRF-TOKEN': csrfToken,
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(data),
});
```

You can also access the CSRF token directly in Twig using the `csrf_token()` function:

```twig
<meta name="csrf-token" content="{{ csrf_token() }}">
```

In any Twig template, you can manually include Vite assets with the `vite_assets()` function:

```twig
{% block head %}
    {{ vite_assets('resources/js/app.jsx') }}
{% endblock %}
```

This emits the correct tags:

- Development: `<script type="module" src="http://localhost:5173/@vite/client">` + your entry script
- Production: `<link rel="stylesheet">`, `<link rel="modulepreload">`, and `<script type="module">` tags

If you use a strict CSP, the development mode (`VITE_DEV_SERVER=true`) may require `connect-src` and `script-src` to allow the Vite dev server. Add this to your CSP policy:

```http
connect-src http://localhost:5173;
script-src http://localhost:5173;
```

The framework's validator includes a built‑in `recaptcha` rule. When applied to the field named `g-recaptcha-response`, it verifies the token with Google's API using your secret key.

```php
public function store(Request $request)
{
    $rules = [
        'name'                 => 'required|alpha_num|min:3',
        'email'                => 'required|email|unique:User',
        'password'             => 'required|min:8|confirmed',
        'g-recaptcha-response' => 'required|recaptcha'
    ];

    if (! $this->validator->validate($request->post(), $rules)) {
        // Redirect back with error messages (flashed to session)
        return $this->redirectBackWithErrors($this->validator->getErrors());
    }

    // Registration logic – only reached if reCAPTCHA is valid
    // ...
}
```

Success: The `recaptcha` rule automatically handles:

- Empty or missing token → validation fails.
- Invalid secret key or network errors → validation fails (logged as a warning).
- Valid token → passes, and you can safely proceed.

If you need to verify the token outside the validator (e.g., in an AJAX endpoint), you can use the static `Recaptcha::verify()` helper directly:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Recaptcha;

$responseToken = $request->post('g-recaptcha-response');
$clientIp      = $request->getClientIp();

if (Recaptcha::verify($responseToken, $clientIp)) {
    // Token is valid
} else {
    // Handle failure
}
```

The `verify()` method performs a POST request to Google's siteverify API using PHP's stream context – no external HTTP client is required.

The `is()` method allows you to check if the request's path matches a given pattern. This is particularly useful for middleware to conditionally apply logic based on the route.

The method supports a trailing wildcard (`*`) to match any subpath:

```php
// Check if the current request is for the API
    if ($request->is('api/*')) {
        // API-specific handling
    }

    // Check for exact match
    if ($request->is('users')) {
        // Users page
    }

    // Check for admin routes
    if ($request->is('admin/*')) {
        // Admin routes
    }
```

The `is()` method normalizes both the request path and the pattern by stripping leading slashes, making it consistent for matching.

```php
// In a controller method
public function updateUser(Request $request, string $id): Response
{
    // Get the request method (e.g., 'get', 'post')
    $method = $request->getMethod(); // "post"

    // Get the clean URI path (e.g., '/users/1')
    $path = $request->getPath(); // "/users/1"

    // ...
}
```

To get a value from the URL's query string (e.g., `/users?page=2`), use the `getQueryParam()` method. It includes a second argument to specify a default value if the parameter is not present.

```php
public function showUsers(Request $request): Response
{
    $currentPage = (int) $request->getQueryParam('page', 1);
    // ...
}
```

Use `allQueryParams()` to retrieve an associative array of all GET parameters from the current request. This is useful when you need to inspect or forward the entire query string.

```php
$params = $request->allQueryParams();
// e.g., ['page' => 2, 'sort' => 'asc', 'filter' => 'active']
```

You can access information about uploaded files using the `getFiles()` method, which returns the contents of the `$_FILES` superglobal.

```php
public function handleUpload(Request $request): Response
{
    $files = $request->getFiles();
    $avatar = $files['avatar'] ?? null;

    if ($avatar && $avatar['error'] === UPLOAD_ERR_OK) {
        // Process the uploaded file
    }

    // ...
}
```

The `view()` helper creates a `Response` object, sets its content to the rendered Twig template, and sets the status code to 200 (OK).

```php
// In a controller
public function home(): Response
{
    return $this->view('home/welcome.twig', ['name' => 'John']);
}
```

The `json()` helper creates a `Response` object, JSON-encodes your data, and automatically sets the `Content-Type` header to `application/json`.

```php
// In an API controller
public function getUser(string $id): Response
{
    $user = $this->userModel->findById($id);

    if (!$user) {
        return $this->json(['error' => 'User not found'], 404);
    }

    return $this->json($user);
}
```

Pair this with `Request::wantsJson()` to let a single route serve both a full page and an AJAX response — see AJAX Pagination.

To redirect a user to another page, Rhapsody uses the `Rhapsody\Core\RedirectResponse` class. The easiest way to create one is with the global `redirect()` helper function.

**Basic Redirects**

```php
public function store(Request $request): Response
{
    // ... save data ...
    return redirect('/users');
}
```

**Redirects with Flash Messages**

Chain the `with()` method to send a flash message for the next request.

```php
return redirect('/users/' . $id)
    ->with('success', 'Profile updated!');
```

This message can be displayed in Twig using the global `flash` variable.

If you need full control, you can create a `Response` instance and set its properties directly:

```php
public function custom(): Response
{
    $response = new Response();
    $response->setStatusCode(201);
    $response->setHeader('X-Custom', 'Hello');
    $response->setContent('Created');
    return $response;
}
```

```php
public function store(Request $request): Response
    {
        // ... save data to the database ...
        return redirect('/users');
    }
```

Chain the `with()` method to send a flash message for the next request:

```php
return redirect('/users/' . $id)
        ->with('success', 'Profile updated successfully!');
```

The `RedirectResponse::send()` method automatically saves the session before redirecting, ensuring flash messages are preserved.

The `Response` object provides getter methods to inspect the response:

- `getContent()` – returns the response body as a string.
- `getStatusCode()` – returns the HTTP status code.
- `getHeaders()` – returns an associative array of headers.

These are especially useful when writing unit tests with PHPUnit.

```php
$response = $this->call('GET', '/about');
$this->assertEquals(200, $response->getStatusCode());
$this->assertStringContainsString('About Us', $response->getContent());
```

The most basic route accepts a URI and a callback. The callback is typically an array containing the controller class and the method name to execute. You can define routes for different HTTP methods like GET and POST.

```php
// routes/web.php
use App\Controllers\PageController;
use Rhapsody\Core\Routing\Router;

// A GET route that shows the about page
Router::get('/about', [PageController::class, 'about']);

// A POST route that handles a form submission
Router::post('/contact', [PageController::class, 'handleContact']);
```

Often, you'll need to capture segments of the URI, such as a user ID or a blog post slug. You can do this by defining route parameters enclosed in curly braces `{}`.

These captured parameters are automatically passed as arguments to your controller method, in the order they appear.

```php
// Route definition
Router::get('/users/{user_id}', [PageController::class, 'viewUser']);

// Controller method
public function viewUser(Request $request, string $user_id)
{
    $user = (new User())->getUserById($user_id);
    // ...
}
```

Important: The router always passes the `Rhapsody\Core\Request` object as the first argument to your controller method, followed by any dynamic route parameters.

When no registered route matches the incoming request, the router doesn't immediately show the 404 page. First, it dispatches a `Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound` event, giving other code one last chance to resolve the URL to something real — most commonly, redirecting a URL that's since moved to a new one.

If nothing is listening for this event, or every listener that is declines to act, behavior is unchanged from a normal 404: an `HttpException` is thrown and handled the usual way. This event only changes anything if something actually listens for it.

```php
namespace App\Listeners;

use Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;

class RedirectLegacyUrls
{
    public function handle(RouteNotFound $event): void
    {
        $newPath = /* look up $event->uri in your own redirects table */ null;

        if ($newPath !== null) {
            $response = (new Response())
                ->setStatusCode(301)
                ->setHeader('Location', $newPath);

            $event->setResponse($response); // tells the router to use this instead of 404ing
        }
    }
}
```

Important: `RouteNotFound` is what the docs call a stoppable event — once a listener calls `$event->setResponse()`, no further `RouteNotFound` listeners run, and the router returns that response directly instead of throwing the 404. This is the exception, not the rule, for how events behave in Rhapsody — see Events & Listeners for the full explanation of stoppable events and why almost no other event works this way.

A module that wants to listen for this event must declare `Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound` under `events.listen.listen` in its `module.json` manifest before it's allowed to — this follows the same permission model as any other module event listener.

You can provide a unique title and meta description for any page by passing a third argument to the `view()` helper in your controller. The framework will automatically use this data to generate `<title>`, `<meta name="description">`, and Open Graph tags.

```php
// In a controller
public function viewUser(Request $request, string $user_id)
{
    $user = $this->userModel->findById($user_id);

    $meta = [
        'title' => $user['name'] . ' - Profile | Rhapsody',
        'description' => 'View the profile for ' . $user['name'] . '.'
    ];

    return $this->view('users/user.twig', ['user' => $user], $meta);
}
```

In your controller, create an instance of `SchemaOrg`, add schemas with the `add()` method, and then render them.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\SEO\SchemaOrg;

$schema = new SchemaOrg();
$schema->add('Article', [
    'headline'      => 'My Great Article',
    'author'        => ['@type' => 'Person', 'name' => 'John Doe'],
    'datePublished' => '2025-01-01',
    'image'         => 'https://example.com/image.jpg',
]);

// Pass the rendered markup to the view
$args['schema_markup'] = $schema->render();
```

If you are extending `BaseController`, the `$this->schema` property is already available, and the `view()` method will automatically inject the rendered schemas into the template as `schema_markup`.

```
// Inside a controller that extends BaseController
$this->schema->add('Product', [
    'name'        => 'Awesome Widget',
    'description' => 'The best widget ever.',
    'sku'         => 'ABC123',
    'offers'      => [
        '@type'         => 'Offer',
        'price'         => '19.99',
        'priceCurrency' => 'USD',
    ],
]);

return $this->view('product.twig', ['product' => $product]);
```

```
use Rhapsody\Core\SEO\Sluggify;

$title = "1 & 2 Samuel: King David's Reign!";
$slug = Sluggify::transform($title);
// Result: '1-2-samuel-king-davids-reign'
```

The transformation steps are:

- Replace `&` with `and`
- Replace non‑letter/digit characters with a single dash
- Transliterate accents to ASCII
- Remove any remaining unwanted characters
- Trim and collapse multiple dashes
- Convert to lowercase

If the result would be empty, it returns `'n-a'` (not available).

Use this helper when generating slugs (or full href links) for blog posts, categories, or any dynamic content:

```
$post = new Post();
$post->title = $request->post('title');
$post->slug = Sluggify::transform($post->title);
$post->save();
```

`pull()` is the right tool for any value that should be used exactly once, such as a one-time confirmation code or the URL a visitor was trying to reach before being sent to the login page:

```php
// Before redirecting to the login form
Session::set('intended_url', $request->getPath());

// After a successful login
$destination = Session::pull('intended_url', '/dashboard');
// 'intended_url' is now gone from the session
```

A flash message is a value that lives just long enough to be shown after a redirect, then disappears by itself. This is the usual “Profile saved” or “Invalid credentials” pattern.

```php
// In the controller that handles the form POST
Session::flash('success', 'Your profile was saved.');
// ...then redirect to the profile page

// In the controller that shows the next page
$success = Session::getFlash('success');   // 'Your profile was saved.' or null
```

Method | What it does
`Session::flash($key, $message)` | Stores a flash message under a key.
`Session::getFlash($key, $default = null)` | Returns the message as a string and removes it immediately. Returns `null` if there is no message, or if the stored value is not a scalar.
`Session::hasFlash($key)` | Returns `true` if a message exists for the key. It does not remove it.

Always regenerate the session ID immediately after a user authenticates. This prevents session fixation, where an attacker plants a known session ID in a victim's browser and waits for them to log in. The old session is deleted.

```php
// credentials verified...
Session::regenerate();
Session::set('user_id', $user->getId());
```

`close()` writes the session data to storage and releases it. Call it before a redirect if you want to be sure everything you set is saved first. Changes made after `close()` are not saved.

```php
Session::set('last_visit', time());
Session::close();
```

The session also holds the CSRF token that protects your forms.

```php
$token = Session::csrfToken();               // created on first use, then reused

if (!Session::verifyCsrfToken($submitted)) {
    // reject the request
}
```

`verifyCsrfToken()` compares the tokens with `hash_equals()`, so the check is safe against timing attacks, and it returns `false` when no token has been issued yet. In templates you rarely call these yourself: put `{{ csrf_field() }}` inside every POST form and it outputs a hidden `_token` input holding the session's token. See Security for the full CSRF workflow.

```json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "id": "20261104-180000-123456-9f3a",
  "captured_at": "2026-11-04T18:00:00.123-05:00",
  "request": {
    "method": "POST",
    "url": "http://localhost/login",
    "headers": {
      "Authorization": "[REDACTED]",
      "Cookie": "[REDACTED]",
      "User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 ..."
    },
    "body": {
      "content": "{\"email\":\"a@b.c\",\"password\":\"[REDACTED]\"}",
      "bytes": 52,
      "truncated": false
    }
  },
  "response": { "status": 422 },
  "queries": {
    "total": 14,
    "recorded": 14,
    "items": [ { "sql": "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?", "params": "[REDACTED]", "time_ms": 0.8 } ],
    "n_plus_one": [ { "count": 9, "total_ms": 6.1, "sql": "SELECT * FROM posts WHERE user_id = 7", "callers": ["app/Models/Post.php:41"] } ]
  },
  "session": { "user_id": "[REDACTED int]", "_csrf": "[REDACTED string]" },
  "exception": null
}
```

The storage and redaction classes are plain, reusable classes in `Rhapsody\Core\Snapshot`:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;
use Rhapsody\Core\Snapshot\Redactor;
use Rhapsody\Core\Snapshot\SnapshotStore;

// The most recent request
$store  = new SnapshotStore(Path::storage('debug/snapshots'));
$latest = $store->latestPath();
$data   = $latest === null ? null : $store->read($latest);

// Newest first
foreach ($store->paths() as $path) { /* ... */ }

// Mask anything of your own before logging or sending it
$safe = (new Redactor(['ssn']))->redactArray($payload);
$text = (new Redactor())->scrubString($message);
```

Add these routes to your `routes/web.php` file:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Controllers\SocialAuthController;

Router::get('/auth/redirect/{provider}', [SocialAuthController::class, 'redirectToProvider'])->name('auth.redirect');
Router::get('/auth/callback/{provider}', [SocialAuthController::class, 'handleProviderCallback']);
```

The `redirectToProvider` method sends the user to the chosen provider's authorization page, and `handleProviderCallback` handles the callback, creating or logging in the user.

The `Rhapsody\Core\Support` namespace holds four small, dependency-free helpers: `Config`, `Env`, `Fs` and `Json`. They wrap the places where PHP hands you `mixed` or `false` (config values, environment variables, file reads, JSON decoding) so your code gets real, typed values, or a clear exception at the exact point something went wrong.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Env;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Fs;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Json;

$settings = Json::decodeArray(Fs::read(Path::config('settings.json')));
$appName  = Env::string('APP_NAME', 'Rhapsody');
```

`Rhapsody\Core\Support\Config` is a read-only, typed reader over your application's config array (the array returned by `config/config.php`). It is registered in the service container as a singleton, so you can type-hint it in a controller constructor or fetch it from the container.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Config;

class MailController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(Environment $twig, private Config $config)
    {
        parent::__construct($twig);
    }

    public function status(Request $request): Response
    {
        $host = $this->config->string('mail.host');
        // ...
    }
}

// Or, anywhere you have the container:
$config = $container->get(Config::class);
```

`Rhapsody\Core\Support\Fs` wraps PHP's file functions so a failure becomes a `RuntimeException` instead of a silent `false`. It pairs naturally with the Path helper, which builds the location.

Method | Returns | Throws when
`Fs::read($path)` | The whole file as a `string` | The file cannot be read
`Fs::lines($path, $flags)` | A list of lines. By default newlines are stripped and empty lines are skipped. | The file cannot be read
`Fs::write($path, $contents, $flags = 0)` | The number of bytes written. Pass `FILE_APPEND` and/or `LOCK_EX` in `$flags`. | The file cannot be written
`Fs::glob($pattern, $flags = 0)` | A list of matching paths. No matches is an empty list, not an error. | The glob itself fails
`Fs::realpath($path)` | The canonical absolute path | The path does not exist
`Fs::cwd()` | The current working directory | It cannot be determined

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Fs;

// Append to a log file
Fs::write(Path::logs('import.log'), date('c') . " import started\n", FILE_APPEND | LOCK_EX);

// Read a file line by line
foreach (Fs::lines(Path::root('.env.example')) as $line) {
    // ...
}

// Find files (an empty list if there are none)
foreach (Fs::glob(Path::storage('exports/*.csv')) as $file) {
    // ...
}
```

```php
try {
    $contents = Fs::read($path);
} catch (RuntimeException $e) {
    // The message names the path, e.g. "Unable to read file: /var/www/..."
}
```

`Fs::read()`, `lines()` and `write()` also suppress PHP's own warning, so a failure produces exactly one exception and no stray warning in your logs or toolbar.

Note: `Fs` does not create missing directories and does not sanitise paths. If any part of a path comes from user input, read the security notes on the Path helper page first.

`Rhapsody\Core\Support\Json` encodes and decodes JSON and always throws a `JsonException` on bad input, so you never have to check `json_last_error()`.

Method | Returns | Notes
`Json::decode($json)` | Any decoded value | Objects become associative arrays. Valid scalars such as `42` or `null` are returned as-is.
`Json::decodeArray($json)` | An `array` | Throws if the document is valid JSON but decodes to a scalar or `null`. Use this when you need an array.
`Json::encode($value, $flags = 0)` | A JSON `string` | Throws if the value cannot be encoded. Any `JSON_*` flags are accepted.

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Json;

$composer = Json::decodeArray(Fs::read(Path::root('composer.json')));
echo $composer['name'];

$payload = Json::encode(['ok' => true, 'url' => 'https://example.com/a/b'], JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES);

try {
    $data = Json::decodeArray($request->getContent());
} catch (JsonException $e) {
    // Not valid JSON, or not a JSON object/array
}
```

Tailwind only generates the classes it finds in your templates, so it needs to know where your Twig files are. Paths in `@source` are relative to the CSS file itself.

```css
/* input.css (application root) */
@import "tailwindcss" source(none);

/* Scan only your theme's templates */
@source "./views/themes/my_theme";

/* Class-based dark mode (see "Dark, Light and System Mode") */
@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));

/* Optional plugins */
/* @plugin "@tailwindcss/typography"; */

/* Your design tokens */
@theme {
    --font-sans: "Inter", ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    --color-brand-500: oklch(0.62 0.19 259);
}
```

`source(none)` switches off Tailwind's automatic file detection so it only scans the paths you list. This keeps builds fast and avoids picking up unrelated files.

Extending core views? Templates in `vendor/arout/rhapsody-core/resources/views` use their own Tailwind classes. If your theme extends or includes any of them (for example the built-in auth views), add `@source "./vendor/arout/rhapsody-core/resources/views";` too. Otherwise their classes won't exist in your compiled CSS and those pages will look unstyled. Automatic detection skips git-ignored folders like `vendor/`, which is why this has to be declared explicitly.

Your `main.twig` must contain the standard Rhapsody blocks (`title`, `description`, `styles`, `head_extensions`, `navigation`, `content` and `scripts`). A minimal Tailwind skeleton looks like this:

```twig
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">

    <title>{% block title %}{{ meta.title }}{% endblock %}</title>
    <meta name="description" content="{% block description %}{{ meta.description }}{% endblock %}" />

    {# Set the dark class before first paint to avoid a flash #}
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                var saved = localStorage.getItem('theme');
                var dark = saved ? saved === 'dark'
                                 : window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
                document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark', dark);
            } catch (e) {}
        })();
    </script>

    {% block styles %}
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ base_url }}/themes/my_theme/css/my_theme.css">
    {% endblock %}

    {% block head_extensions %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body class="bg-white text-gray-900 dark:bg-gray-950 dark:text-gray-100">

    {% block navigation %}
        {# your header / nav #}
    {% endblock %}

    <main>
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    </main>

    {% block scripts %}
        {{ vite_assets('resources/js/app.jsx') }}
        {{ schema_markup|raw }}
    {% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
```

A few points worth noting:

The `@custom-variant` line in `input.css` makes `dark:` utilities respond to a `dark` class on the `<html>` element, and the inline script in `main.twig` sets that class before the page paints. A toggle only needs to flip the class and remember the choice:

```javascript
function setTheme(mode) {          // 'light' | 'dark' | 'system'
    var root = document.documentElement;
    if (mode === 'system') {
        localStorage.removeItem('theme');
        root.classList.toggle('dark', window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
    } else {
        localStorage.setItem('theme', mode);
        root.classList.toggle('dark', mode === 'dark');
    }
}
```

If you'd rather follow the operating system only, skip the custom variant. Tailwind's default `dark:` behaviour uses `prefers-color-scheme`.

Tailwind finds classes by scanning your files as plain text, so it can't see classes assembled at render time:

```twig
{# Won't work: Tailwind never sees "bg-red-500" #}
<div class="bg-{{ color }}-500">

{# Works: full class names appear in the source #}
{% set colors = {
    'danger':  'bg-red-500',
    'success': 'bg-green-500'
} %}
<div class="{{ colors[status] }}">
```

Rather than growing a pile of custom CSS, put repeated UI (buttons, cards, form fields) in Twig partials and include them:

```twig
{# views/themes/my_theme/partials/button.twig #}
<button type="{{ type|default('button') }}"
        class="rounded-lg bg-brand-500 px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-white
               hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2">
    {{ label }}
</button>

{# Usage #}
{% include 'partials/button.twig' with { label: 'Save', type: 'submit' } %}
```

On v3 the setup uses a config file instead of CSS directives:

```bash
npm install -D tailwindcss@3
npx tailwindcss init
```

```javascript
// tailwind.config.js
module.exports = {
    darkMode: 'class',
    content: [
        './views/themes/my_theme/**/*.twig',
        // './vendor/arout/rhapsody-core/resources/views/**/*.twig',
    ],
    theme: { extend: {} },
    plugins: [],
};
```

```css
/* input.css */
@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;
```

```bash
npx tailwindcss -i ./input.css -o ./public/themes/my_theme/css/my_theme.css --minify
```

The `phpunit.xml` file is already provided in the project root. It points to `tests/bootstrap.php` as the bootstrap file. Make sure your `bootstrap.php` defines the `ROOT_DIR` constant and loads Composer's autoloader:

```php
<?php
// tests/bootstrap.php
define('ROOT_DIR', dirname(__DIR__));
require_once ROOT_DIR . '/vendor/autoload.php';
```

This file is executed before every test run, ensuring that your application's classes are autoloaded and any global constants are defined.

To prevent test data from interfering between tests, use the `RefreshesDatabase` trait. It provides a `setUpDatabase()` method that drops and recreates all tables based on your Doctrine entity metadata.

```php
namespace Tests\Feature;

use Rhapsody\Core\Testing\TestCase;
use Rhapsody\Core\Testing\Traits\RefreshesDatabase;

class UserTest extends TestCase
{
    use RefreshesDatabase;

    protected function setUp(): void
    {
        parent::setUp();
        $this->setUpDatabase(); // Rebuilds schema
    }

    public function test_user_can_be_created(): void
    {
        $user = new User(['email' => 'test@example.com']);
        $this->app->get('entityManager')->persist($user);
        $this->app->get('entityManager')->flush();

        $this->assertNotNull($user->getId());
    }
}
```

Performance Tip: For large test suites, consider using transactions instead of full rebuilds. The trait can be extended to support transaction‑based rollbacks – check the framework's advanced testing guide.

The `call()` method dispatches a request through the router and returns a `TestResponse` object. This wrapper offers a fluent interface for common assertions, making your tests more readable.

```php
public function test_homepage_returns_successful_response(): void
{
    $this->call('GET', '/')
         ->assertOk()                  // status 200
         ->assertSee('Welcome to Rhapsody')
         ->assertDontSee('Error');
}

public function test_login_validation_fails(): void
{
    $response = $this->call('POST', '/login', [
        'email' => 'invalid',
        'password' => 'short',
    ]);

    $response->assertStatus(302)       // redirect
             ->assertSessionHasErrors(['email', 'password']);
}
```

Available assertion methods include:

- `assertOk()`, `assertStatus($code)`
- `assertSee($text)`, `assertDontSee($text)`
- `assertJson(array $data)`, `assertJsonMissing(array $data)`
- `assertRedirect($uri)`
- `assertSessionHas($key, $value)`, `assertSessionHasErrors()`

Two helper methods (`swap()` and `mock()`) allow you to substitute real services with test doubles.

- `swap($abstract, $instance)` – replaces the container's binding for the given interface or class with your own object.
- `mock($abstract, callable $mockDefinition)` – creates a PHPUnit mock, lets you configure expectations, and then swaps it into the container in one step.

```php
public function test_payment_flow(): void
{
    // Swap with a custom fake
    $fakeGateway = new FakePaymentGateway();
    $this->swap(PaymentGatewayInterface::class, $fakeGateway);

    // Or create a mock with expectations
    $this->mock(PaymentGatewayInterface::class, function ($mock) {
        $mock->expects($this->once())
             ->method('charge')
             ->with(100.00)
             ->willReturn(true);
    });

    $this->call('POST', '/checkout', ['amount' => 100])
         ->assertOk()
         ->assertSee('Payment Successful');
}
```

This approach allows you to test controller logic without hitting external APIs or relying on real implementations.

When testing forms that include a reCAPTCHA widget, you can mock the validation rule or the `Recaptcha` helper to always return success. For example:

```php
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Recaptcha;

public function test_registration_with_recaptcha(): void
{
    // Make the verify() method always return true
    $this->mock(Recaptcha::class, function ($mock) {
        $mock->method('verify')->willReturn(true);
    });

    $this->call('POST', '/register', [
        'name' => 'John Doe',
        'email' => 'john@example.com',
        'password' => 'secret123',
        'password_confirmation' => 'secret123',
        'g-recaptcha-response' => 'dummy-token', // still required, but validation passes
    ])->assertRedirect('/dashboard');
}
```

Alternatively, you can disable the rule entirely in your test environment by using a conditional validation rule (e.g., only apply `recaptcha` if `APP_ENV !== 'testing'`).

If you want to keep the framework’s default design (Tailwind styling, layout structure, and helpers) but change specific parts, you can extend the core templates.

Use the `@core` namespace in your Twig templates to reference core views. For example:

```twig
{% extends "@core/layouts/main.twig" %}

{% block content %}
    <!-- Your custom content here -->
{% endblock %}
```

This approach inherits the core layout, styles, and global variables (like `base_url`, `auth`, `flash`, etc.). You can override any block defined in the core layout.

Tip: The core design uses Tailwind CSS v4.3. If you extend `@core/layouts/main.twig`, you automatically get these styles and the Vite asset pipeline.

```twig
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{% block title %}My Custom Site{% endblock %}</title>
    <meta name="description" content="{% block description %}{% endblock %}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/my-styles.css">
    {% block styles %}{% endblock %}
    {% block head_extensions %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body class="{% block body_class %}{% endblock %}">
    {% block navigation %}<header>My Header</header>{% endblock %}

    {% block full_width_content %}{% endblock %}

    <main class="{% block main %}{% endblock %}">
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    </main>

    <footer>My Footer</footer>
    <script src="/js/app.js"></script>
    {% block scripts %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
```

You can then extend your own layout in any child template:

```twig
{% extends "layouts/main.twig" %}
{% block content %}
    <!-- custom content -->
{% endblock %}
```

This is the single most common mistake when building a theme, or a page within one, and unlike the missing-block problem above, nothing catches it automatically — it fails exactly as silently as Twig's default behavior for blocks always does.

When `layouts/main.twig` puts something meaningful inside a block — a script tag, a stylesheet link, anything — any child template that overrides that same block completely replaces that content unless it explicitly calls `{{ parent() }}` first. This is ordinary Twig inheritance behavior, not a Rhapsody-specific rule, but it's easy to forget precisely because the failure is invisible: the page still renders, it just quietly lost whatever the parent put there.

The most consequential real example: the React bridge is typically wired up with `{{ vite_assets('resources/js/app.jsx') }}` placed inside `layouts/main.twig`'s `scripts` (or `head_extensions`) block. Any page template that overrides that same block to add its own inline script — a hero slider, a checkout form, anything — will silently break every React island on that specific page if it doesn't call `parent()` first:

```twig
{% extends 'layouts/main.twig' %}

{% block scripts %}
    {{ parent() }}  
    <script>
        // this page's own script
    </script>
{% endblock %}
```

In your controller, you can instantiate the validator, define your rules, and then check if the data passes. If validation fails, you can easily get the error messages and pass them back to the view.

```php
// In a controller method, e.g., handleContact()
use Rhapsody\Core\Validator;

$data = $request->getBody();
$validator = new Validator();

$rules = [
    'name' => 'required|min:2|max:50',
    'email' => 'required|email',
    'password' => 'required|min:8|confirmed',
    'avatar' => 'required|image|mimes:jpeg,png'
];

if ($validator->validate($data, $rules)) {
    // Data is valid, proceed with your logic...
} else {
    // Data is invalid, re-render the form with errors.
    return $this->view('your_form.twig', [
        'errors' => $validator->getErrors(),
        'old' => $data // Pass back the old input
    ]);
}
```

You can print data passed from your controller using double curly braces: `{{ ... }}`. Twig automatically escapes this output to prevent XSS attacks, making it secure by default.

```php
// In your controller
return $this->view('profile.twig', ['username' => 'Alice']);

{# In profile.twig #}
<h1>Welcome, {{ username }}!</h1>
```

Flash messages are temporary messages that are stored in the session and displayed once, then automatically cleared. You can create them using the `->with()` method when returning a redirect response:

```php
// In your controller
public function submitForm(Request $request): Response
{
    // ... process form data ...

    if ($success) {
        return redirect('/dashboard')->with('success', 'Your profile has been updated!');
    } else {
        return redirect('/profile')->with('error', 'There was a problem updating your profile.');
    }
}
```

The `redirect()` helper returns a `RedirectResponse` object, and `->with()` accepts two arguments: the flash message type and the message content.

Flash messages are automatically available in all Twig templates via the `flash` global variable. The main layout template already includes a section to display `success` and `error` messages.

You can display them manually in any template like this:

```twig
{% if flash.success %}
    <div class="alert-success">{{ flash.success }}</div>
{% endif %}

{% if flash.error %}
    <div class="alert-error">{{ flash.error }}</div>
{% endif %}
```

The main layout file `@core/layouts/main.twig` already includes this logic, so you don't need to add it to every template. However, you can add additional flash types (like `warning` or `info`) by creating them in your controller and displaying them in your templates accordingly.

You're not limited to `success` and `error`. You can use any key you want:

```php
// In your controller
return redirect('/dashboard')->with('warning', 'Your session is about to expire.');
return redirect('/dashboard')->with('info', 'A new update is available.');
```

And in your template:

```twig
{% if flash.warning %}
    <div class="alert-warning">{{ flash.warning }}</div>
{% endif %}

{% if flash.info %}
    <div class="alert-info">{{ flash.info }}</div>
{% endif %}
```

💡 Tip:

Flash messages are one-time use. After they are displayed, they are automatically cleared from the session. This makes them perfect for showing feedback after form submissions, logins, or other actions.

